      "text": "{\"response\":\"Logical Reasoning Guide to\\nQuestion and Reasoning Types\\n\\nQuestion Types 2\\nAgree/Disagree 2\\nFlaw 3\\nMain Conclusion 6\\nMethod 8\\nMost Supported 10\\nMust be True 12\\nMust be False 14\\nNecessary Assumption 17\\nParadox 20\\nParallel 22\\nParallel Flaw 24\\nPrinciple-Strengthen 26\\nPrinciple-Conform 27\\nRole 28\\nStrengthen 30\\nWeaken 31\\n\\nReasoning Types 33\\nCausation 33\\nConditional Logic 35\\nComparison 36\\nExplain Curious Fact 39\\nFamous Flaws 41\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nQuestion Types\\n\\nAgree/Disagree\\n\\nKeywords\\nDisagree, agree, point at issue\\n\\nExamples\\nThe dialogue above most supports that Pam and Dawn disagree about which of the following?\\nBased on their statements, Jerome and AJ are committed to agreeing to which of the following?\\nWhich of the following is the point at issue between Martinez and Howie?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\nThe first person\u0027s statements more often resemble an argument, while the second person\u0027s often don\u0027t have any explicit conclusion. Ultimately, though, we don\u0027t care which claim is a background vs. premise vs. conclusion. We\u0027re just hunting for the claims that clash / match.\\nMost of the time, the claim they agree/disagree with is explicit. Only on harder ones will we have to ask ourselves about whether their point of (dis)agreement is an assumption.\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\nWe want to try to dial this in pretty specifically. Since we are usually locating one and only one claim in Person 1\u0027s statements that Person 2 (dis)agrees with, we can be very proactive about looking for answer choices that sound like that claim.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n1. Read each person\u0027s statements (Don\u0027t worry about finding their \\\"conclusion\\\" or \\\"support\\\")\\n2. After you\u0027ve read each person\u0027s paragraph, go back and revisit each claim the 1st person made.\\n3. For each claim, ask yourself if the 2nd person agreed / disagreed / we don\u0027t know.\\n4. If there aren\u0027t any explicit claims that the 2nd person is agreeing / disagreeing with, then ask yourself whether the 1st person is assuming anything the 2nd person would agree/disagree with.\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\nOn a Disagree or Point at Issue task, the correct answer is a claim for which we can\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ninfer that one of the people would agree and the other person would disagree (they would agree to its logical contradiction).\\n\\nOn an Agree question, the correct answer is a claim for which we can infer that each person would agree.\\n\\nFrequently, the correct answer uses the phrasing of one person, to express the opinion of the other person. For example, if the first person says, \\\"Rigatoni is the most traditional form of pasta\\\" and the second person disagreed by saying, \\\"Hogwash. Spaghetti predates rigatoni by two centuries\\\", then the answer would either...\\n\\nUse person 1\u0027s language to express person 2\u0027s opinion:\\n\\\"Rigatoni is not the most traditional form of pasta\\\"\\n\\nOr use person 2\u0027s language to express person 1\u0027s opinion:\\n\\\"Spaghetti does not predate rigatoni by two centuries\\\"\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n• Both Agree - On disagree questions, there are often answers that both people agreed to or would presumably agree to\\n• Both Disagree - On agree questions, there is often one answer that shows where the two people disagreed\\n• Out of Scope - The most common trap answer brings up a topic that one (or both) of the people never addressed.\\n• Too Strong - This can be tricky to see because the Too Strong version of a lot of trap answers is the contradiction. Remember, for a correct answer, we can infer that one person would agree, and we can infer that the other person would agree to the logical contradiction (the negation).\\n\\nSo if we see an answer like:\\n• (A) Some attempts by the government to intervene are more problematic than the problem they attempted to solve.\\n\\nIt\u0027s easy to believe that one person would agree to that weakly worded claim. But would the other person disagree, i.e. would they agree that \\\"Attempts by the govt to intervene never make the situation more problematic than before\\\"? Probably not.\\n\\nFlaw\\n\\nKeywords\\nVulnerable to criticism, flaw, questionable reasoning, errors of reasoning\\n\\nExamples\\nThe argument is most vulnerable to criticism because it ...\\nFred commits which of the following errors of reasoning?\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nThe publicist\u0027s reasoning is flawed because she ...\\n\\nStimulus tendencies\\nThese are arguments that either have a noteworthy Assumption, are vulnerable to a certain sort of Objection, or make some sort of Dubious / Illicit Move.\\n\\nThere are about a baker\u0027s dozen Famous Flaws that show up a lot on these problems, especially within the answer choices:\\n\\n• Necessary vs. Sufficient - Taking a conditional relationship and but then illegally thinking through it in backwards or opposite fashion.\\n• Causal - Overconfidently concluding one possible causal interpretation of a curious fact, usually a Correlation, when other possible interpretations exist • Part vs. Whole - Assuming that a trait that is true of each Part of a collective is therefore true of the Whole, or vice versa.\\n• Unproven vs. Proven False - Pointing out that someone failed to prove X is true and then concluding that this means that X is false.\\n• Ad Hominem - Dismissing a point of view because the source has a biased interest or has conflicting past behavior.\\n• Sampling - Extrapolating/generalizing too readily from a small sample, while failing to consider that the sample might be biased, too small, or unrepresentative.\\n• Irrelevant Rebuttal - Rebutting someone\u0027s claim or argument by mischaracterizing it in some Straw Man way or by bringing up something wholly irrelevant to the original claim.\\n• False Choice - Acting like you have to choose between two options, as though they\u0027re a binary / exhaustive set, even though you never established those are the only options.\\n• Relative vs. Absolute - Getting mixed up with Percents vs. Amounts, or confusing more/less language with yes/no language.\\n• Inappropriate Appeal - Your evidence is a plea for sympathy, you cite an \\\"authority\\\" without establishing credibility, or you favor one source\u0027s opinion over another source\u0027s, without justifying your pick.\\n\\nAlmost always wrong answers:\\n• Circular Reasoning - The premise of an argument restates or assumes the conclusion.\\n• Internal Contradiction - Something the author says undermines or contradicts something else they say\\n• Equivocation - Using the same \\\"term\\\" or concept to mean two very different things.\\n\\nReading Goal\\nAt its simplest, Flaw just wants us to read some confused blowhard\u0027s crappy argument and say, \\\"Objection, Dr. Stupid did this.\\\" Thus, try to read the argument and respond with an objection to the reasoning. Is there a distinction the author isn\u0027t perceiving? Is something too strong? Is something irrelevant? Does the author\u0027s thinking feel like it\u0027s cheating in some way?\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSince the answers will be doing so, we should feel free to speak any of three languages. \\\"The argument is flawed because ...\\n\\n• ... the author assumes X\\\" (Assumption answers)\\n• ... isn\u0027t it possible that Y is true?\\\" (Objection answers)\\n• ... the author made this illegal move\\\" (Description answers)\\n\\nOne framework that usually works is, \\\"Just because your Premise is true, doesn\u0027t mean your Conclusion is true\\\". You might not realize you\u0027re reading a Part to Whole flaw, but you can help your brain by saying, \\\"Just because each member of the team is efficient doesn\u0027t mean that the team will be efficient\\\".\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\nYou can always ask two questions of the correct answer:\\n• Is this descriptively accurate? (If not, eliminate)\\n• Is this a logical problem with going from Premise to Conclusion? (if not, eliminate)\\n\\nDepending on the phrasing of the answer choice, you can also pose one of these three questions to yourself:\\n• Was the author Assuming that, or is this Too Strong / Out of Scope? • Would this be an Objection, or is it Too Weak / Irrelevant to the Conclusion? • Does this abstract wording Match the Premise and the Conclusion/Assumption?\\n\\nASSUMPTION phrasings\\ntakes for granted / presumes / assumes / fails to establish ... that\\nX OBJECTION phrasings\\nfails to consider / ignores / neglects / overlooks the possibility ... that\\nY MATCH phrasings\\nconcludes C on the basis of P / infers, on the grounds that P, that C / confuses P with C\\ntakes for granted that C because P / fails to consider that even if P, may not be C\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n• Too Strong - The assumption answers are very often too strong. So many wrong answers accuse the author of taking for granted some strongly worded claim. A lot of them also say the author concluded X merely on the basis of Y. That means that Y was the only shred of evidence, and that\u0027s frequently not the case.\\n• Bad Match - So many answers are descriptively inaccurate. Either the premise language in the answer doesn\u0027t resemble the actual evidence, the conclusion language in the answer doesn\u0027t resemble the actual conclusion, or both. As soon as you find a mismatching part, you should bail from that answer.\\n• Reversed / Opposite wording - Lots of answers have the right ideas in the wrong order\\n• Wrong Flaw - Almost 50% of the answers in Flaw go back to the top ten Famous\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nFlaws, so we\u0027re routinely seeing old friends like Sampling and thinking, \\\"This wasn\u0027t one of those.\\\"\\n• Contradicted - It says the author \\\"failed to consider\\\" something she definitely considered.\\n\\nMain Conclusion\\n\\nKeywords\\nMain conclusion, main point\\n\\nExamples\\nWhich of the following best expresses the main conclusion of the argument? Which of the following is Eddie\u0027s main point?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\nLSAC is seeking to make it harder than usual to find the Conclusion. So it\u0027s very rare to see the Conclusion be the final claim, prefaced by anything obvious like Therefore / Thus / So, though sometimes it will be that.\\n\\nRather than making the conclusion the last claim, in more than 90% of Main Conclusion questions, the conclusion appears, then evidence comes after.\\nThese are the two most common places they stash the conclusion:\\n• First sentence\\n• After a But / Yet / However pivot.\\n\\nOther times, the Conclusion will be indirectly revealed by Support language like \\\"for, after all, to see this\\\". They usually wouldn\u0027t use an obvious Premise indicator like \\\"since\\\" or \\\"because\\\".\\nSometimes, our best clue about where the conclusion is will be an Opinion Indicator like \\\"probably, apparently, evidently\\\".\\n\\nBeware that there may be Intermediate Conclusions. They will pass the Support test because they will have their own premise. But they are there to support the Main Conclusion.\\nBeware that there may be Causal-Indicator words that look like Conclusion-Indicator words. You can use \\\"thus, therefore, hence, so\\\"\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\nThis is the most",
