      "createTime": "2026-07-10T22:53:49.803Z"
    }, {
      "text": "{\"response\":\"lovable question type ever. We just have to find the conclusion and reiterate to ourselves, \\\"Okay, don\u0027t overcomplicate this --- just go find that claim we identified as the conclusion\\\". Many conclusions will be some form of rebuttals or other claim that uses referential language (pronouns, like \\\"this, that, they, such\\\")\\n\\nIf so, we want to take the time to replace the pronoun with its reference language.\\nF.e. Some people propose laws that would require mandatory background checks before owning a gun. However, such laws won\u0027t affect the homicide rate. Almost all homicides are committed by people who would pass the background check proposed in this law.\\nIf we correct identified the 2nd sentence as the conclusion, our job is to remind ourselves what \\\"such laws\\\" refers to and use that language in its place when we\u0027re saying the conclusion back to ourselves.\\n\\\"Okay, the conclusion is that [laws requiring mandatory background checks] won\u0027t affect the homicide rate.\\\"\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the conclusion.\\n2. Make sure that it\\n3. Sounds like Author\u0027s Opinion (facts ≠ conclusions)\\n4. Is Supported by at least one explicit claim.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nThe correct answer just reproduces the meaning of the Conclusion. Sometimes it\u0027s a verbatim regurgitation. More commonly, they use synonyms or inverted syntax to present an equivalent meaning that still looks a little different.\\n\\nF.e. If the conclusion appeared in the argument as \\\"not all tornadoes should be avoided\\\", then an equivalent claim would be \\\"some tornadoes should not be avoided\\\".\\nIf you didn\u0027t already do the 2-part Conclusion Check when you were first reading the argument and determining which claim is the conclusion, you can do it now (or again)\\n--\\n• Is this answer expressing an Opinion from our Author? (facts ≠ conclusions) •\\nWas this answer Supported by at least one explicit claim?\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Premise\\n• Last Claim Trap - They know that we wish for the last claim to be the Conclusion •\\nIntermediate Conclusion\\n• What Next - These present a claim that was never said, but they don\u0027t like the\\nnext thing the author would probably say\\n• Necessary Assumption - These feel tempting because we can tell that they are a\\ncorrect inference about the author\u0027s thinking, but the Conclusion is an explicit\\nclaim, whereas these assumptions are implicit; they were never said\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nMethod\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nProceeds by, responds by, argumentative technique, method of reasoning\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe diplomat\u0027s argument proceeds by ...\\nCharlie responds to Sarah in which of the following ways?\\nWhich of the following is a technique\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThere\u0027s a pretty consistent list of argument moves that they test us on:\\n\\n• Analogy\\n• Rules Out Competing Options\\n• Implications of Logic\\n• Make a Distinction\\n• Alternate Explanation for a phenomenon\\n• (Counter)example to a General Principle\\n• Point Out Dubious Assumption\\n• Raise an Overlooked Consideration\\n\\nYou don\u0027t have to memorize this list, but if you become more familiar with it, you may\\nfind it easier to anticipate possible answer wording. Here\u0027s a quick example of each of\\nthese:\\n\\n• Analogy - Parents want to force their children to practice the piano. But just as\\nyou can\u0027t force the blossom of a rose before it\u0027s ready, we should give children\\nthe time they need to come to the piano on their own terms.\\n• Rules Out Competing Options - Eliza wants her child Herbert to learn an\\ninstrument. Herbert is only interested in drums, piano, and flute. Since drums and\\npiano are too big to fit inside Eliza\u0027s apartment, she should buy Herbert a flute.\\n• Implications of Logic - Some claim that we should always be honest with\\npeople. But if this were true, then when my daughter plays me the new song she\\njust \\\"wrote\\\" on piano and asks me if I like it, I would be needlessly discouraging\\nher if I honestly told her that I thought it was hot garbage.\\n• Make a Distinction - Many parents who live in apartment building are worried\\nabout their children practicing a musical instrument at home and annoying the\\nnearby tenants. However, it is primarily low frequency sounds that penetrate\\nwalls, due to their longer waveforms. Thus, a flute, which is primarily a high frequency instrument, is fine to practice in an apartment building, as its sound is\\nunlikely to disturb neighbors.\\n• Alternate Explanation for a phenomenon - The rise in music recordings that use\\nsampled drums, rather than live drums, has been attributed to the ascendancy\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nof hip-hop as one of the most popular styles of music. However, it is also\\npossible that more music makers are turning to sampled drums because\\nsound-editing programs like Garage Band, which makes adding sampled drums\\nto a recording very easy, have become standard software in many computers.\\n• (Counter)example to a General Principle - It\u0027s often said that you never get a\\nsecond chance to make a first impression. The first night Sheila met her eventual\\nhusband, she said a lot of embarrassing things. However, he was so blackout\\ndrunk at the time, that when he saw her at a mutual friend\u0027s party a month later,\\nhe didn\u0027t realize he had ever met her before.\\n• Point Out Dubious Assumption - MARK: When I visited my niece a year ago,\\nshe was really into those Twilight books, so for Christmas I\u0027m getting her a\\nTwilight calendar. LEROY: Children\u0027s entertainment obsessions change on an\\nalmost-quarterly basis. Are you sure she\u0027s still into Twilight?\\n• Raise an Overlooked Consideration - ABDUL: Sharon ended up getting a 152 on\\nher LSAT, which is only a 50th percentile score, so I guess that tutor she hired\\nwas a waste of money. SARAFINA: She had been stuck at 140 for months until\\nshe started working with the tutor, and now she has a score that could get her\\ninto law school.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\nIf we\u0027re reading one person\u0027s argument:\\n\\n\u003d1. Find the conclusion.\\n2. Find the support.\\n3. Try to characterize the evidence (analogy, counterexample, citing an authority,\\netc.) or the way that the author arrived at her conclusion (ruling out other options,\\nlooking at the implications of a statement the opponent/the author makes, etc.)\\n\\nIf we\u0027re reading two people\u0027s statements and describing the response:\\n\\n1. Read both paragraphs\\n2. Ask yourself whether the 2nd person was adding onto the 1st person\u0027s argument\\nor pushing back against something.\\n3. If they were pushing back (much more common), were they arguing against the\\nevidence (basically never happens), arguing against an assumption, accepting\\nthe evidence and arguing for a difference conclusion, or just creating some doubt\\nby raising alternative possibilities or overlooked considerations?\\n4. Did the second person actually conclude anything, or just provide food for\\nthought?\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\nIf you can match up the argument you\u0027ve read with anything from the list of common\\nanswers (analogy, example, distinction, alternative explanation, etc.) then cue up that\\nlanguage. About 1/3 of these deal with analogies. Otherwise, be okay letting the answer\\nchoices guide you. It\u0027s often very hard to predict the wording of the eventual answer\\nchoice.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nThe correct answer doesn\u0027t say anything descriptively wrong. The other four do. Much\\nof what we have to do when analyzing these answer choices is match up one abstract\\nphrase with the evidence and another with the conclusion. Pay attention to wording\\nindicating which part of an answer deals with Conclusion, which deals with Evidence,\\nand which deals with an Opposing position.\\n\\n• Concludes C on the basis of E\\n• Infers, from the claim that E, that C\\n• Attempts to C by E\\n• Demonstrates C by E\\n• Claiming C because of E\\n• Basing a conclusion on the claim that E\\n• Refuting O on the grounds that E\\n\\nAnswers choices describing a 2nd person\u0027s response often just make one claim:\\n\\n• Disputes X\\n• Criticizes X\\n• Provides X\\n• Agrees with X but argues Y\\n\\nIncorrect Answer\\n\\nThere aren\u0027t really patterns or types of correct answers, because they\u0027re all wrong for\\nbeing descriptively inaccurate about something. The one frequently recurring wrong\\nanswer on Describe the Response type tasks, though, is that the 2nd person responded\\nby \\\"denying the validity of the evidence\\\". We know that\u0027s not LSAT\u0027s style. The 2nd\\nperson never does that.\\n\\nMost Supported\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nStatements, information, passage, most support which answer, which answer is most\\nsupported, most illustrates\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nIf the statements are true, they most strongly support which of the\\nfollowing? The information given most supports which one of the following?\\nWhich of the following propositions is best illustrated by the situation described?\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese are almost always FACTS, not ARGUMENTS (when they are an argument, the\\ntested inference is usually very similar to a necessary assumption). The stimulus\\nfrequently features some blend of Conditional Logic, Causality, Comparisons, Pivots,\\nor Quantified language\\n\\nConditional Logic - We might have to chain together multiple conditionals, and/or we\\nmight be able to apply a conditional rule to a provided fact (the fact usually triggers the\\ncontrapositive).\\n\\nCausality - There are some chains of causality that let us infer that First thing causes\\nLast thing (A causes B, which leads to C let us infer that A leads to C). There are also\\nsome examples of Solve the Causal Mystery, in which the most supportable answer is\\na speculative guess about what could account for the facts. Most commonly, there is a\\nCausal Difference-Maker identified or suggested. Explicit causal language looks like\\nbecause of this / due to this / this allows / this makes possible / this leads to. Implicit\\ncausality is more like \\\"Thing A and B are similar when it comes to X, but different when\\nit comes to Y. So, if they are different when it comes to Z, then Y was probably the\\ncausal difference-maker that accounts for Z\\\".\\n\\nComparisons / Contrasts - Many Fill in the Blank questions (Logical Completion) have\\na complete the analogy feel. They tell us that \\\"Thing A is like Thing B. And since X\\nwould be true of Thing A, _____ \\\", setting us up for answer that reinforces \\\"X would be\\ntrue of Thing B\\\".\\nA lot of comparison/contrast ideas are setups for correct answers that reinforce the\\nCausal Difference-Maker. When the paragraph Ends with a Pivot, i.e. the last idea is\\nintroduced with a but / yet / however pivot, the correct answer usually safely reconciles\\nthe last idea with what came before it.\\n\\nQuantified / Mathematical - These are much more common on Must Be True\\nquestions, but if we see wording like \\\"some, most, all, few\\\", then the correct answer\\nmight be recognizing that two qualities overlap at least",
