k language in answers, focusing on terms like \"some,\" \"can,\" and \"may.\" Also, I\u0027m watching for ruling-out language using \"not,\" particularly within verbs, in order to rule out facts. This weak phrasing supports the best answers. I\u0027ll remember the need for a softer approach in Necessary Assumption problems.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Constraints**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on typical errors. I noted that words such as \"most,\" \"typically,\" and \"generally\" often signal incorrect answers. I\u0027m also watching for \"Out of Scope\" assumptions, and backwards logic. \"Fake Opposite\" answers are an additional trap.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Paradox**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on \"Explain\" questions, specifically where a paradox or discrepancy exists. I\u0027m noting the paradox often involves causality or comparisons between differing factors. The goal is to identify the causal difference-maker and resolve the apparent conflict. I\u0027m focusing on the examples, such as why an objective wasn\u0027t achieved.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Explanations**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on \"Explain\" questions, but these are distinct from those involving paradoxes. I understand they simply present a situation requiring explanation. In the question stems, the explicit reference to what needs explanation helps to guide the focus. I\u0027m noting that paradox-based questions often use the terms \"paradox,\" \"conflict,\" or \"discrepancy.\"\n\n\n**Interpreting the Explanations**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on \"Explain\" question strategies. I\u0027m noting that paradox-style questions involve causal or comparative discrepancies, aiming to find the causal difference-maker. I\u0027m also watching for that common preface, like \"However.\" The solution reconciles the surprising claim against a background fact. I\u0027m watching for new information.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Question Types**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on \"Explain\" questions that don\u0027t present a paradox, understanding their focus on requiring a situation. Also, I\u0027m now learning that paradoxes are about causality or comparisons, where I seek the difference-maker.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Stimuli**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on analyzing stimulus patterns, noting a valid, logical framework. It seems 70-75% of arguments use conditional logic, with the remainder using other forms. I\u0027m noting specific argument templates, such as if A then B; B then C; and if X then Y or Z. I\u0027ll pay attention to these.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Argument Structures**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on logical structures, noting Z has happened. I\u0027m focusing on the \"X is B\" inference when \"X is A or B,\" and A is improbable. I\u0027m considering \"Most A\u0027s are B, Most A\u0027s are C,\" so \"Some B\u0027s are C\u0027s\" implications. I\u0027m also watching for \"If X, then Y,\" leading to \"not X\" if \"not Y.\" I\u0027m now noting common patterns like doomed actions and past-future predictions.\n\n\n**Defining the Framework**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on defining claim types, including conditional, either/or, and normative statements. I\u0027m also focusing on quantified, comparative, and causal language, and statements of fact. I\u0027m differentiating claims based on their certainty: certain, probable, and possible assertions. I will be mindful of these when considering pre-phrasing.\n\n\n**Refining the Strategy**\n\nI\u0027m now rehearsing the abstract recipe, seeking a must-have element for easy scanning, and thinking, \"Which is easiest to confirm?\" I want a jumping-off point to assess potential answers quickly, ensuring the same number of premises. This process helps disqualify answers and expedite evaluation.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Conclusion**\n\nI am now assessing the claim in the answer\u0027s structure, focusing on maintaining a similar strength of claim. It\u0027s common for premise order to shuffle, but the content must be complete. When I get stuck, I will relax my constraints. Also, I\u0027m watching for traps where the topic may be nearly the same as in the original claim.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing the Logical Errors**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying common logical errors in arguments. I\u0027m noting the importance of matching the type and strength of claims in parallel flaw questions. I\u0027m focusing on \"Bad Conclusion Matches,\" where the claim type or strength is wrong, or the reference is incorrect. I\u0027m also watching out for \"Validity Mismatches\" in answers.\n\n\n**Identifying the Famous Flaws**\n\nI\u0027m now cataloging logical fallacies for application. \"Failure to Consider Alternative Explanations,\" \"Sampling,\" \"Ad Hominem,\" and \"Unproven vs. Proven False\" are now identified. I\u0027m focusing on their signatures in arguments. My strategy includes diagramming quantified arguments for structure. I\u0027m also preparing for objections, especially to \"Neither\" scenarios or point-of-view choices.\n\n\n**Refining the Framework**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the framework for pre-phrasing responses, given a specific flaw. The argument must have the same core structure. I\u0027m focusing on the number of claims and what they include. I\u0027m focusing on conditional, quantified, and other claims. I\u0027m analyzing the elements within the flaw itself.\n\n\n**Refining the Focus**\n\nI am now focusing on aligning the correct answers with the flaw, matching structural elements. I\u0027m noting the need to soften constraints when answers are elusive, or to be picky when faced with near-matches. The correct answer has to replicate the same flaw, or be vulnerable to the same objection as the original argument. I am ready to differentiate the subtleties to clarify the answer.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Question Type**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying common flaws in answers, such as those with missing premises. I\u0027m focusing on \"Bad Premise Matches,\" where the ingredient count is off, and also \"Bad Conclusion Matches,\" where the claim is the wrong type. \"Validity Mismatches\" are also key. I am focusing on this for the principle-strengthen concept.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Reasoning**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on a specific question type involving principles. I\u0027ve noted two main variations: one with principles in the stimulus, the other with principles in the answers. I am focusing on connecting the conclusion to principles. I need to justify arguments using principles.\n\n\n**Refining the Principles**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on refining the principles-based question strategies. I\u0027m noting the importance of pre-phrasing the conclusion using \"IF premise, THEN conclusion.\" I\u0027ll ensure the correct answer balances premise and conclusion elements. I\u0027m also prioritizing elimination of answers that reverse the logical direction. Also, I\u0027ll identify how interests are prioritized, and where that comes into play. I\u0027m focusing on strengthening principles, confirming application triggers, and conforming with principles.\n\n\n**Applying the Logical Principles**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how implied principles work and how they relate to conditional rules. I\u0027m noting the need to find answers where the trigger in the stimulus matches the principle\u0027s conclusion, even when not explicit. I\u0027m focusing on strengthening principles, confirming application triggers, and conforming with principles. I also plan to focus on principles that come from answer choices.\n\n\n**Defining the Strategy**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on \"Principle-Conform\" questions, seeing similarities to parallel reasoning. It\u0027s about finding the underlying rule in an argument and matching it to an answer. I\u0027m noting the need to articulate the author\u0027s guiding principle, not diagramming structures. Identifying the role of key claims and statements is now my primary focus.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Argument**\n\nI am now focusing on understanding the devil\u0027s handmaiden\u0027s role, noting the LSAC seeks to obscure conclusions. I\u0027m focusing on identifying final claims, intermediate conclusions, and support language. I\u0027m paying attention to opinion indicators and the need to detect intermediate support.\n\n\n**Defining the Core Elements**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on defining argument components, differentiating the Main Conclusion from supporting claims and intermediate conclusions. Also, I must classify claims as supporting, neutral, or opposing the author. I also aim to quickly pre-phrase answers using core labels like \"Conclusion,\" \"Supports,\" or \"Opposes\". Finally, I\u0027m noting the importance of choosing answers that avoid descriptive errors.\n\n\n**Defining the Language**\n\nI\u0027m now clarifying the key language. I\u0027m focusing on \"Main Conclusion\" as the main point and \"Premise\" as supporting evidence. I\u0027m distinguishing between \"Intermediate Conclusion,\" which supports the main one, and \"Opposing Position,\" which is challenged. I\u0027m noting common error types and their descriptions.\n\n\n**Strengthening the Focus**\n\nI am now categorizing \"Strengthen\" questions, noting common keywords like \"most strengthens,\" \"supports,\" and \"reasoning.\" I\u0027m also observing stimulus tendencies, focusing on causal, comparative, and predictive arguments. These often involve interpretations of correlations, similarities, and projections of the future. I\u0027m focusing on strengthening conditional ideas to make the answers work.\n\n\n**Refining the Pre-Phrase**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on strengthening arguments, particularly causal and comparison-based ones. I am now looking to add plausibility to hypotheses and bolster comparison arguments, anticipating evidence that supports the author\u0027s claims. For example, ruling out alternate explanations and establishing connections between elements are both key.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Focus**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on evaluating \"Strengthen\" and \"Weaken\" question strategies. I\u0027m focusing on incorrect answer types, such as those that weaken or have no impact. I\u0027m also watching for \"Too Weak\" options that only use qualifying language. Then, I am focusing on what makes a correct answer: providing missing logical links, making fair comparisons, and ruling out alternatives.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Goal**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on the core goal: finding the conclusion and the supporting evidence. I\u0027m also planning to challenge these with an anti-conclusion. I\u0027m watching for term shifts to spot answers that try to disconnect them.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Question**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying the causal and comparative elements inherent to the question type. I\u0027ll search for alternate explanations or causal mismatches in support of an answer. The comparative arguments will be assessed for meaningful differences between the items in question.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Argument Types**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying common argument types related to causality. I\u0027m focusing on \"Curious Fact\" and causal conclusions in Strengthen/Weaken questions, as well as Flaw arguments that jump to conclusions from correlations. I\u0027m also noting the importance of considering alternate explanations. In Necessary Assumption questions, the need to assume plausible causal storylines is also key. In Must Be True / Most Supported questions, I\u0027m noting the presence of causal chains.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Causal Arguments**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on causal relationships, particularly how X causing Y, and Y causing Z, leads to X causing Z, or that reducing X would reduce Z. I\u0027m focusing on \"Most Supported\" questions and how to recognize causal difference-makers. \"Because of this\" and similar phrases will trigger ideas that things like X cause things like Y. I\u0027m noting parallel flaws may use causal flaws. In Paradox questions, I will look for storylines to account for how things could be different.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Causal Factors**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on causal relationships. I\u0027m focusing on \"Causal Difference-Maker\" elements to explain variations between groups or periods. I am noting keywords like \"causes\" and \"leads to\" to help identify these causal links. Also, I\u0027m watching for those phrases that denote causal connections within the text, such as \"Because of this.\"\n\n\n**Analyzing the Argument\u0027s Framework**\n\nI am now categorizing arguments with curious facts. I will be focused on causal explanations where I can propose alternative interpretations of data. I\u0027m noting the importance of considering the plausibility of the author\u0027s claims and how they present their data. I can now evaluate how different options can support or refute interpretations.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Causality**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how causal arguments function across question types. I\u0027ve noted that weakening arguments often introduce alternative explanations, or demonstrate a lack of cause/effect. Strengthening arguments bolster plausibility by showing cause/effect relationships or by ruling out alternative explanations. I\u0027m noting that flaw questions emphasize the assumption of causality based on correlation.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Implications**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on conditional logic\u0027s impact, particularly the frequency of its influence on incorrect answers and its presence across logical reasoning and logic games. I\u0027m recognizing its relevance within stimulus and answer choices. I\u0027m noting the struggle some test-takers have, emphasizing the need for specific skills.\n\n\n**Categorizing the Conditional Focus**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on question type categorization within the framework of conditional logic. I\u0027m noting specific types where conditionals are central: Necessary Assumption, Most Strongly Supported, and Flaw questions (particularly when testing the Necessary vs. Sufficient flaw). I will also focus on recognizing conditional indicators like \"if,\" \"only,\" and \"must,\" translating claims into \"X → Y\" and their contrapositives.\n\n\n**Chaining the Conditionals**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how to string conditionals together for complex problems. My notes cover how to make logical chains, such as if \u0027A\u0027 implies \u0027B\u0027 and \u0027B\u0027 implies \u0027C\u0027, then \u0027A\u0027 implies \u0027C\u0027. I\u0027ll be working to understand and look for indicators to build a strong answer.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Analogies**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on analyzing arguments by comparison. I will be looking for common weaknesses within comparisons, especially assumptions about similarity that may not be valid. I am focusing on making the strength of the arguments stronger or weaker by demonstrating similarity or dissimilarity, like Jacob being into 90\u0027s Hip-Hop, or the LSAT and marathon similarities.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Assumptions**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on comparative arguments, particularly those assuming similarity. I\u0027m noting examples like LSAT and marathons, recognizing that a seemingly small difference, like the ease of mental recovery versus physical, can undermine the comparison. I can strengthen by establishing relevant differences, or weaken by demonstrating the opposite. I\u0027m focusing on advertising campaigns, and how this relates to my process.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Assumptions**\n\nI am now focusing on the given examples, specifically the \"Past vs. Present\" and \"Present vs. Future\" arguments. For both, I\u0027m developing strategies to strengthen and weaken them by considering factors that create or negate past-to-present continuity, and considering the presence or absence of factors that may influence what could happen in the future.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Trends**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on strengthening or weakening trend-based arguments. I can strengthen them by providing more reasoning, and by showing the trend continuing; I can weaken by introducing something that\u0027ll disrupt this trend. A novel element can throw things off, or something that we\u0027re overlooking can be the reason. I\u0027m noting the importance of considering these elements.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Counterfactuals**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on a new thought about Ivan and Crosby. I am considering potential counterfactuals. If Crosby already had a high number of matches, Ivan\u0027s equal count suggests that the photographer was effective, thus supporting an argument. I also note that factors unrelated to the photo may still impact the result.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Strategy**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on a case study involving Crosby and Ivan, and how to analyze their photo match comparisons. I\u0027m noting the importance of establishing a baseline and considering the effect of external variables like previous matches. I will also be considering a new example that involves percentage vs. amount in terms of student age, with pros and cons also considered.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Examples**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on strengthening/weakening arguments with examples. I\u0027m noting the Baxter vacuum argument; I can strengthen it if Reggie values rug settings. Alternatively, I can weaken it if Reggie doesn\u0027t prioritize settings, or if the Baxter has disadvantages. Also, I\u0027m noting how examples and analogies work for the logical completion types.\n\n\n**Expanding the Arguments**\n\nI am now focusing on completing analogies and strengthening/weakening arguments around causal explanations. I\u0027ll consider how \"stretching tendons\" relates to an opera, or how we might strengthen an author\u0027s explanation by ruling out alternatives. I am looking at how things not exposed to a cause can relate to an argument, or conversely, how alternatives can suggest other explanations, in strengthening and weakening scenarios.\n\n\n**Defining the Flaw Types**\n\nI\u0027m now clarifying the common patterns in flaw and necessary assumption questions. I\u0027ve noted that flaw questions often highlight overconfidence in a causal link or failure to consider alternate explanations. Conversely, necessary assumption questions frequently involve ruling out alternate explanations to support the author\u0027s argument, or establishing facts necessary for plausibility. I\u0027ll focus on the \"curious fact\" to identify the crux of the argument.\n\n\n**Questioning the Stimulus**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on converting facts into inquisitive prompts to uncover underlying causal factors or explanations. My goal is to transform statements, like the revenue decrease or the purple catfish\u0027s behavior, into \"why\" questions to better understand potential triggers or assumptions. I\u0027m focusing on \"Jim\" and his drinking.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Causal Storylines**\n\nI am now focusing on dissecting and fully explicating author\u0027s causal reasoning, particularly where the story\u0027s steps are implied. For example, in the \"Jim\" scenario, the implied steps were Jim getting into an accident because he was driving while drunk. I must now focus on fully understanding each causal sequence. I\u0027m also ready to analyze the implied steps in the LawHub scenario, and in the cricket wax example.\n\n\n**Categorizing the Answers**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying how to categorize correct answers in \"Strengthen\" and \"Weaken\" questions. I\u0027ll focus on whether answers suggest or rule out alternate explanations, or if they increase or decrease the plausibility of the author\u0027s argument. I will now classify the role of the correct answer. I am focusing on how an answer may rule out an alternate explanation.\n\n\n**Defining the Flaw Types**\n\nI\u0027m now cataloging the \"Necessary vs. Sufficient\" fallacy. It\u0027s often the root of flaws. Also, I\u0027m noting conditional reasoning where incorrect assumptions are made by interpreting backwards or in the opposite way. I\u0027ll need to know this, and watch for this in answers. I understand these are frequent flaws in stimuli for Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Claims**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying logical fallacies in arguments, specifically overconfident causal conclusions and part-to-whole assumptions. I will differentiate cases of casual-based curious facts and correlation to interpret the details. For example, some may think watching the documentary is the only way to understand the prison system, but I can also consider the military example.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Reasoning**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on logical fallacies, specifically \"Unproven vs. Proven False\" and \"Ad Hominem\". I\u0027m noting examples of each fallacy, and focusing on recognizing their signatures in arguments, such as the Elvis uncle example and the Atlanta Braves argument. I will now examine these flawed claims with a focus on bias and correlation.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Logical Elements**\n\nOkay, I\u0027m now zeroing in on logical fallacies related to bias in the arguments. I\u0027m focusing on those relating to bias, such as the governor\u0027s flip-flop, the social worker\u0027s industry and the Republican theater examples. I am now going to define the impact of these in arguments. I will be on the lookout for flawed claims that rely on these.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Examples**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on recognizing and cataloging more logical fallacies, specifically \"Irrelevant Rebuttal\" and \"False Choice.\" I will be creating flashcards with these, including example scenarios to aid in quick identification. I also intend to construct my own examples of each flaw for review and feedback.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Fallacies**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying common fallacies like False Dilemma, where limited options are presented. I\u0027m noting specific examples, such as the Mario Kart/rope swing dilemma. I\u0027ll also be watching for Inappropriate Appeal, and inappropriate applications of authority. I\u0027m focusing on Elon Musk\u0027s tweet.\n\n\n",
