        "thought": true
      }, {
        "text": "**Analyzing the Claims**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying logical fallacies in arguments, specifically overconfident causal conclusions and part-to-whole assumptions. I will differentiate cases of casual-based curious facts and correlation to interpret the details. For example, some may think watching the documentary is the only way to understand the prison system, but I can also consider the military example.\n\n\n",
        "thought": true
      }, {
        "text": "**Evaluating the Reasoning**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on logical fallacies, specifically \"Unproven vs. Proven False\" and \"Ad Hominem\". I\u0027m noting examples of each fallacy, and focusing on recognizing their signatures in arguments, such as the Elvis uncle example and the Atlanta Braves argument. I will now examine these flawed claims with a focus on bias and correlation.\n\n\n",
        "thought": true
      }, {
        "text": "**Analyzing the Logical Elements**\n\nOkay, I\u0027m now zeroing in on logical fallacies related to bias in the arguments. I\u0027m focusing on those relating to bias, such as the governor\u0027s flip-flop, the social worker\u0027s industry and the Republican theater examples. I am now going to define the impact of these in arguments. I will be on the lookout for flawed claims that rely on these.\n\n\n",
        "thought": true
      }, {
        "text": "**Analyzing the Examples**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on recognizing and cataloging more logical fallacies, specifically \"Irrelevant Rebuttal\" and \"False Choice.\" I will be creating flashcards with these, including example scenarios to aid in quick identification. I also intend to construct my own examples of each flaw for review and feedback.\n\n\n",
        "thought": true
      }, {
        "text": "**Analyzing the Fallacies**\n\nI\u0027m now identifying common fallacies like False Dilemma, where limited options are presented. I\u0027m noting specific examples, such as the Mario Kart/rope swing dilemma. I\u0027ll also be watching for Inappropriate Appeal, and inappropriate applications of authority. I\u0027m focusing on Elon Musk\u0027s tweet.\n\n\n",
        "thought": true
      }],
      "createTime": "2026-07-10T23:24:16.698Z"
    }, {
      "text": "{\"response\":\"once, or that one group must be bigger than another.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. As we read facts, we\u0027re looking for two or more ideas that we could synthesize. Look for clues about where that synthesis might come, by keeping your eyes peeled for Conditional, Causal, Math-y, or Comparison / Contrast language. In particular, look out for overlapping ideas. An idea that shows up more than once is often the conduit to chaining something together, triggering a rule, or otherwise making an inference.\\n2. If you see a Pivot, think about what it would sound like to safely combines what comes after the pivot with what came before.\\n3. If it\u0027s Logical Completion (fill in the blank), think about a safely worded conclusion that would pull together the thoughts already mentioned.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nThe most provable claim there is always the correct answer. Sometimes it\u0027ll feel 99% must be true. Other times it will feel more speculative, but it\u0027s always the most supported. It does not need to use all the facts; in fact, it\u0027s a very common template that the inference involves combining the first and last claims (while the middle claim acts as filler).\\n\\nCorrect answers will often have easy-to-prove language like \\\"some, could, can, may, might, sometimes, not always, not all, need not\\\".\\n\\nIt\u0027s very rare for the correct answer to be derived from only one idea, so you should normally feel like in order to support the correct answer, you need to combine 2 or more facts.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Too Strong - Words like \\\"all, none, only, unless\\\", as well as medium-strong words like \\\"typically, usually, most, generally, tends to, primarily\\\" often are stronger than what the information has told us.\\n• Out of Scope - Answers that bring up things for which we were provided no information are almost always wrong\\n• Illegal Reversals / Negations - If there is any conditional logic involved, there will usually be at least one trap answer that represents the conditional logic in backwards or opposite fashion.\\n• Unknown Comparisons - A lot of correct answers take an absolute adjective like \\\"successful\\\" and try to make a comparative claim like \\\"more successful\\\" out of it.\\n• Contradicted - Some answers actually go against what we read.\\n\\nMust be True\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nStatements, information, passage, must be true, properly inferred, follows logically\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nIf the statements are true, which one of the following must be true?\\nWhich of the following can be inferred by the information in the passage?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese are FACTS, not ARGUMENTS.\\nThe stimulus usually features some blend of Conditional / Quantified ideas.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nConditional Inferences\\n\\nWe often have to chain together multiple conditionals.\\nWhen we have a chain of ideas, such as A → B → C → D, the most likely correct answer would be A → D or the contrapositive ~D → ~A. But any subset of that chain can be properly inferred, such as A → C or B → D.\\nThe other common setup is that we have a fact that triggers a conditional rule (usually by contrapositive) and lets us infer the outcome of the rule.\\nWe may have 2 or 3 possible options identified, but through the course of ruling out all but 1 option, we are forced to infer that final option.\\n\\nQuantified / Mathematical Inferences\\n\\nIf we see words like \\\"some, most, all, few\\\", then the correct answer might be testing that two qualities overlap at least once, or that one group must be bigger than another.\\n\\nMost A\u0027s are B ....... Most A\u0027s are B\\nMost A\u0027s are C ....... Most B\u0027s are ~A\\nSome B are C ........More B\u0027s than A\u0027s\\n\\nAll A\u0027s are B ....... Most A\u0027s are B\\nSome A\u0027s are C ......All B\u0027s are C\\nSome B are C ....... Most A\u0027s are C\\n\\nThere are other mathematical ideas tested, relating to % change, averages, and inequalities.\\n\\n• If x \u003e y, and y \u003e z, then x \u003e z.\\n• If the avg score for Group A was 160, and the avg score for the non-teachers in the group was 152, then the avg score for the teachers in the group had to be higher than 160.\\n• If my rent is higher now than five years ago, but my rent represents that same proportion of my salary as it did five years ago, then my salary now is higher than five years ago.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\nAs we read facts, we\u0027re looking for two or more ideas that we could synthesize. Look for clues about where that synthesis might come, by keeping your eyes peeled for Conditional, Quantified, or otherwise Mathematical ideas.\\n\\nIf you see Conditional language, look for a fact that triggers the rule (or, more likely, that triggers the contrapositive). If you have more than one Conditional, see if they chain together.\\n\\nIf you see a Pivot holding two mathematical ideas in tension, think about how to mathematically reconcile those two ideas.\\n\\nIf you see words like \\\"Some / Most / All\\\", ask yourself if you have two MOST facts about\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nthe same group. If so, you can infer some overlap to the two qualities mentioned.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nIt is provable based on only the provided information. It does not need to use all the facts; in fact, it\u0027s a very common template that the inference involves combining the first and last claims (while the middle claim acts as filler). It\u0027s very rare for the correct answer to be derived from only one idea, so you should normally feel like in order to support the correct answer, you need to combine 2 or more facts.\\n\\nWe would gravitate more towards answers with easy-to-prove language like \\\"some, could, can, may, might, sometimes, not always, not all, need not\\\". However, on Must Be True it is very common for the correct answer to have strong, black-and-white language. Since almost all the inferences come from Conditional or Mathematical thinking, they have the certainty that justifies strong language.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Too Strong - Words like all, none, only, unless, as well as medium-strong words like typically, usually, most, generally, tends to, primarily often are stronger than what the information has told us.\\n• Out of Scope - Answers that bring up things for which we were provided no information are almost always wrong\\n• Reversed / Opposite logic - If there is any conditional logic involved, there will usually be at least one trap answer that represents the conditional logic in backwards or opposite fashion.\\n• Unknown Comparisons - A lot of trap answers take an absolute adjective like \\\"successful\\\" and try to make a comparative claim like \\\"more successful\\\" out of it.\\n• Contradicted - Some answers actually go against what we read.\\n\\nMust be False\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMust be false, incompatible, violates, most justifiably rejected\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nIf the statements are true, which one of the following must be false? Based on the statements, each of the following could be true EXCEPT: Which of the following is LEAST COMPATIBLE with the information in the passage? Which of the following situations would violate the principle stated? The passage provides the strongest reason for rejecting which of the following?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese are FACTS, not ARGUMENTS.\\nMust Be False question stems will usually have fact sets that include Conditional or Mathematical ideas.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nMost Strongly Opposed question stems will usually have fact sets that feature a Similarity, Distinction, or Causal Difference-Maker.\\n\\nConditional\\n\\nIf we have more than one conditional, chain them together.\\nIf we get a conditional and some fact about X applies to the trigger or to the contrapositive\u0027s trigger, then infer the outcome about X.\\nThe correct answer will either contradict a conditional or conditional chain, by providing a case in which the trigger is true but the outcome isn\u0027t, or it will contradict an inference we derived from applying a conditional rule to fact X.\\n\\nMathematical\\n\\nIf we see words like \\\"some, most, all, few\\\", then the correct answer might be testing that two qualities overlap at least once, or that one group must be bigger than another.\\n\\nMost A\u0027s are B ........ Most A\u0027s are B\\nMost A\u0027s are C ........ Most B\u0027s are ~A\\nSome B are C .........More B\u0027s than A\u0027s\\n\\nAll A\u0027s are B ....... Most A\u0027s are B\\nSome A\u0027s are C ....... All B\u0027s are C\\nSome B are C ........ .Most A\u0027s are C\\n\\nThere are other mathematical ideas tested, relating to % change, averages, and inequalities.\\n\\n• If x \u003e y, and y \u003e z, then x \u003e z.\\n• If the avg score for Group A was 160, and the avg score for the non-teachers in the group was 152, then the avg score for the teachers in the group had to be higher than 160.\\n• If my rent is higher now than five years ago, but my rent represents that same proportion of my salary as it did five years ago, then my salary now is higher than five years ago.\\n\\nA correct answer would simply contradict whatever mathematical inference we\u0027re able to make.\\n\\nCausal Difference-Maker\\n\\nWhen Inference passages give us causal language, it factually establishes a causal influence. Look out for claims that get connected with language like\\n\\n• Because of this\\n• Due to this\\n• This allows\\n• This makes possible\\n• By doing this\\n• This leads to\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n• Resulting in\\n• Contributes to\\\".\\n\\nExample: Patrick started chewing gum during his timed sections. By doing this, his score improved.\\nFrom that setup, it would be Must Be False-ish to say stuff like, \\\"New routines never lead to improvement\\\" or \\\"The activities of your jaw are wholly independent of your performance on a test.\\\"\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\nRead this as though you\u0027re trying to make a valid inference, because they will often contradict a valid inference that can be made. Pay particular attention to whether there is any Conditional language. Can conditionals be chained together or can a rule be applied to a fact to derive a 2nd fact? If there\u0027s not any conditional logic, then there\u0027s likely a mathematical inference that can be made or a causal difference-maker situation discussed.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nIt is CONTRADICTED by the information in the passage (or in Least Compatible / Most Justifiably Rejected, it is badly undermined by something in the passage).\\n\\nIf it\u0027s contradicting a conditional rule, it will establish that the trigger of the rule is happening, but the outcome is not happening. If it\u0027s contradicting an inference we made, it\u0027ll just sound like a negation of that inference or an impossibility, given the truth of that inference.\\n\\nThe correct answer is often strongly worded, because contradicting a strong statement requires something very weak. (if you say \\\"all ice cream is delicious\\\", I can contradict you with just one batch of Cousin Larry\u0027s Meatballs \u0026 Marshmallows ice cream).\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Weak Language - It\u0027s hard to contradict a weak claim, because you\u0027d have to prove something really strong. If someone said, \\\"Some cops abuse their power\\\", it would be hard to contradict them, since \\\"some\\\" is so weak and so contradicting it would require arguing that \\\"no cops ever have abused their power\\\".\\n• Out of Scope - Answers that bring up things for which we were provided no information are almost always wrong. We can\u0027t contradict an idea if we never covered that idea.\\n• Reversed / Opposite logic - There are illegal inferences, but that\u0027s not the same thing as a contradiction. Students frequently confuse Must Be False with \\\"this can\u0027t be inferred\\\". Don\u0027t conflate Must Be False with Unsupported. We\u0027re saying there IS support, for the OPPOSITE of this answer.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nNecessary Assumption\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nAssumption, depends, relies, requires, must be assumed, assumes, presupposes, takes for granted\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?\\nThe author assumes which of the following?\\nThe argument relies on which of the following assumptions?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nNecessary Assumption is a real mixed bag. Probably 60% of them are testing us on Idea Math, seeing if we notice a missing piece in the author\u0027s logic, similar to what we\u0027re doing on Sufficient Assumption and Principle-Justify questions. The other 40% are really testing us on Possible Objections, seeing if we can think of a way to accept the author\u0027s evidence but reject her conclusion, similar to what we\u0027re doing on Flaw, Strengthen, and Weaken.\\n\\nWhen we see Conditional logic, other chains of ideas, or repeated ideas, (i.e. going to a museum calms your soul, and a calm soul finds it easier to think. thus, going to a museum makes you more likely solve a difficult problem), that\u0027s usually a strong sign that the correct answer will simply connect ideas that were only mentioned once (you\u0027re more likely to solve a difficult problem if it\u0027s easier to think).\\n\\nWhen we see arguments that are using Comparison or Causal reasoning structures, then it\u0027s more likely that the correct answer is testing our ability to see where potential objections could arise. When an argument is comparative in nature, the author often needs to assume two things are relevantly similar or needs to assume there\u0027s a meaningful difference between the option they\u0027re choosing and the one they\u0027re rejecting. When an argument is causal in nature (i.e. the author\u0027s conclusion involves one possible causal explanation for some curious background fact), the author needs to assume that her explanation achieves minimal standards of plausibility and, assuming the author\u0027s conclusion sounds pretty sure of itself, the author needs to assume that there is not some other way to explain the background fact.\\n\\nIf an author is rebutting someone\u0027s position, she needs to assume that the reason she offers for her rebuttal actually conflicts with the position she\u0027s trying to reject. If an author is rejecting a conditional, she needs to provide a situation where the trigger occurred, but the outcome did not.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nNegating Answers\\n\\nOne of the most reliable ways to get Necessary Assumption answers correct is to get good at the Negation Test: the correct answer, if negated, badly weakens the argument. An incorrect answer, if negated, doesn\u0027t weaken the argument. Negating a claim means contradicting it in the most minimal fashion. The negation of \\\"All my friends are honest\\\" isn\u0027t \\\"None of my friends are honest\\\", it\u0027s \\\"at least one of my friends is not honest\\\". There are a few standards to go by when negating:\\n\\n• Don\u0027t negate every single answer as you read them. Try to get rid of some for easier reasons like Out of Scope or Too Strong wording. Use the negation test more to confirm the answer you\u0027re picking.\\n• If the truth value of an answer hinges on a quantity word, f.e. \\\"Some clowns are scary\\\", then negate the quantity term. Know your quantity yin-yangs (Some vs. None / All vs. Not all / Most vs. Not-Most, i.e. 51% vs. 49% / Many vs. Not-Many / Sometimes vs. Never)\\n• If the truth value hinges on the word not, then just remove the not, f.e. negating \\\"Taking the vacation will not deplete Luke\u0027s savings\\\" gives us \\\"taking the vacation will deplete his savings\\\".\\n• If the truth value hinges on the verb, negate the verb, f.e. negating \\\"Washing dishes dries out your hands\\\" gives us \\\"Washing dishes does not dry out your hands\\\".\\n• If the answer is conditional, don\u0027t negate it. Negating a conditional does not give you a conditional. If gives you one counterexample, in which the trigger happens but the outcome does not. Instead of negating conditional answers, just mentally or physically diagram the If / Then statement and ask yourself if the author\u0027s argument made that move. Most of these will be wrong because they\u0027ll be Backwards or Opposite logic.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the Conclusion.\\n2. Find the Support.\\n3. Ask yourself if there\u0027s a New Term or idea in the Conclusion. (If so, that idea will almost always be in the correct answer)\\n4. If there is any conditional logic, or chain of ideas, or repeated ideas, then this problem is probably testing a missing link or fact. If there is conditional logic, mentally or physically diagram it. Link conditionals together where possible. Think about the missing conditional piece you would need to get from the Subject of the conclusion (Eddie) to the Predicate (is a great friend).\\n5. If there isn\u0027t conditional logic, switch into debate mode: GIVEN the evidence, HOW COULD YOU ARGUE the anti-conclusion?\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\nBecause of the mixed bag nature of Necessary Assumption arguments, we\u0027ll find that sometimes we have a very specific idea we\u0027re looking for (need to join \u0027easier to think\u0027 with \u0027solve difficult problem\u0027). Other times we\u0027ll have a functional goal for the answer, but not specific wording. If an author is relying on a comparison where two things are\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nsaid to be similar, we might think, \\\"Let\u0027s look for an answer that rules out a significant difference between the two things\\\". If an author is concluding that X is the explanation for some Curious Fact, we might think, \\\"Let\u0027s look for an answer that rules out an alternate explanation or that provides some fact that must be true for X to be a plausible explanation\\\".\\n\\nNecessary Assumptions are the opposite of Objections. If we would object to an author, \\\"hey, maybe those two people are twins!\\\", then we can say the argument was assuming \\\"those two people are not twins\\\". If we could tell an author was assuming \\\"Jack likes cookies\\\", then we could object to that argument by saying \\\"Jack doesn\u0027t like cookies\\\". So in some cases, we read a Necessary Assumption paragraph and can already hear something the author is assuming. In those cases, that\u0027s our pre-phrase. Other times, we don\u0027t hear an assumption, so we switch into debating mode and try to think of a possible objection. If we come up with Objection X, then we can pre-phrase, \\\"Okay, the author is assuming that Objection X is not true.\\\"\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\nThere are a few different ways to think about what makes a Necessary Assumption answer correct. They\u0027re all valid in all cases, but you may find that it\u0027s easier to process a correct answer using one formulation vs. another, depending on the problem:\\n\\n• Which answer, when negated, most weakens the argument?\\n• Which answer can clearly be derived from the author\u0027s thinking?\\n• Which answer shows a move that the author clearly made?\\n\\nThe first one is well suited to answers with the word \\\"not\\\" in them, that are ruling out Objections. The second one is well suited to answers that are patching together language (like \\\"easier to think\\\" is relevant to \\\"solve difficult problem\\\"), or to answers that assume a relevant similarity or that assume a basic plausibility to an author\u0027s hypothesis. The third one is well suited to conditional logic answer choices.\\n\\nWeak language is our friend. Correct answers frequently have watered down phrasings like \\\"some, sometimes, can, may, might, not all, not always\\\". Ruling-out language is also our friend. Many correct answers use the word not, next to the verb, to rule out some fact.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Too Strong / Specific - We\u0027re accusing the author of assuming the answer we pick, so we don\u0027t want to accuse them of something harsh or crazy sounding (unless they sounded that crazy, and they usually don\u0027t). Words that imply certainty are conditional, so we can evaluate those answers by comparing their conditional logic to the reasoning flow of the argument. But much of the time we can just hear that an idea is way too strong for the author to have committed herself to defending. Just like in Most Supported and Reading Comp, words like most, typically, usually, tends to, generally, primarily, rarely are usually wrong.\\n• Out of Scope - We\u0027re not speculating on what else the author might believe. If we didn\u0027t talk about it, then the author hasn\u0027t made any assumptions about it. (The one exception being that in causal arguments the author will be assuming\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nthat some other explanation that we didn\u0027t talk about is not the actual causal explanation)\\n• Opposite / Backwards Logic - Lots of wrong answers are conditional logic relationships that don\u0027t go from Prem to Conclusion; they go from Conclusion to Premise, or they assume that Not-Premise leads to Not-Conclusion. There are lots of Fake Opposite answers that also just flip a premise. If an author said that \\\"vinegar-based salad dressings are low in fat\\\", a trap answer will say he\u0027s assuming that \\\"non-vinegar-based salad dressings are high in fat\\\". We can\u0027t do that.\\n• Weakens - Some wrong answers are actually objections. The correct answer, when negated, should weaken. But an answer, as stated, should never weaken.\\n\\nParadox\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nExplain, resolve, paradox, discrepancy, apparent conflict\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following, if true, most helps to explain the data from the study?\\nWhich of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThe paradox usually hinges around Causality and Comparisons.\\nWe are usually needing to figure out the Causal Difference-Maker between two things, two groups, two situations, two timeframes.\\n\\n• Given this sameness, how are they different?\\n• Given this difference, how are they the same?\\n• Given this positive, how is this seen as a negative?\\n• Given this negative, how is this seen as a positive?\\n• Given this plan, how come its objective wasn\u0027t achieved?\\n• Given X\u0027s alibi, how can we explain why Y happened?\\n\\nSome Explain questions feel a little different. They don\u0027t necessarily pit two things in tension with each other. They just present something and ask us to Explain it. But these are 15% or less of Paradox questions.\\n\\nOn Explain question stems, you\u0027ll always see a specific reference to what we\u0027re trying to explain. If it says, \\\"Which of the following, if true, most helps to explain Sarah\u0027s reaction to the gift\\\", then we already know that everything else is background, and our job is simply to pick an answer that makes sense of Sarah\u0027s reaction.\\n\\nOn Resolve question stems, they\u0027re more likely to just use a generic term like \\\"paradox, apparent conflict, discrepancy\\\", so then it\u0027s more on us to figure out where the tension is (although half of the tension will always be the final claim).\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Understand the paradox. These are not arguments, so do not look for a Premise or Conclusion.\\n2. Find the Surprising Claim. It\u0027s almost always the final idea, and it\u0027s almost always prefaced by a pivot word like However / Yet / Nevertheless.\\n3. What Background Fact(s) makes that a surprise? Ask yourself, \\\"Why would we have expected something different from this last, surprising claim?\\\"\\n\\nFrame the tension\\n\\nGIVEN the background claim(s), HOW CAN I EXPLAIN the surprising claim?\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nIt gives us some way to make sense of the surprising claim or unexpected difference.\\n\\nThe stronger the wording, the more impact an answer potentially has, so this is a question type where we Prefer Stronger Language, and where we could consider Weak Language \u003d Red Flag (sometimes, can, may, might, not all, not always).\\n\\nWhenever a question stem says, \\\"Which of the following, if true, most... \\\", (Paradox, Strengthen, and Weaken), a few things apply:\\n\\n• We expect brand new things we haven\u0027t heard before\\n• We prefer stronger language over weaker language\\n• We will have to use a little bit of outside knowledge / common sense to connect what the answer is saying to the impact it has on the stimulus\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Opposite - These actually increase the surprise\\n• Explains Background - These tell us more about why the background fact is true, but they don\u0027t help us understand why the surprising claim is true\\n• No Distinction - This is special trap answer that we see on Paradox; since we are almost always looking for some meaningful difference between two things / two groups / two timeframes / two situations, if an answer provides something that would be true for both things, for both situations, then it\u0027s useless to us.\\n• Too Weak - It\u0027s possible for an answer to lose out to the correct answer because it\u0027s worded much more weakly.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nParallel\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMost similar, parallels, pattern of reasoning, structure of reasoning\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe pattern of reasoning in the argument is most similar to which of the following?\\nThe pattern of reasoning in which of the following arguments most closely parallels that in the argument?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese will (almost always) be valid, or at least highly reasonable arguments. We don\u0027t need to push back on the argument when we evaluate it, although recognizing that an original argument is 100% valid can make it that much easier to get rid of any answer choice whose argument is flawed.\\n\\nSince the LSAT is testing patterns of structure, the arguments need a lot of Conditional or Quantified signposts, so that we can successfully represent them abstractly. About 70-75% of these arguments will contain Conditional Logic. The other 25-30% of them can be abstractly captured by some principle like, “if it ain’t broke, don’t fix it\\\", or by some familiar LSAT reasoning pattern like \\\"Just because two things are correlated, doesn\u0027t mean that one causes another, because they could both be effects of some third thing\\\".\\n\\nHere are some of the more common argument templates.\\n\\n• All A\u0027s are B. All B\u0027s are C. X is an A. Thus, X is a C.\\n• If X, then Y or Z. X happened. Y didn\u0027t happen. Thus, Z happened.\\n• X is A or B. A doesn\u0027t seem possible. Thus, X is probably B.\\n• Most A\u0027s are B. Most A\u0027s are C. Thus, Some B’s are C’s.\\n• If X, then Y. But Y isn\u0027t true / isn\u0027t good. So, not X / shouldn\u0027t do X.\\n\\nHere are some of the principles / patterns they\u0027ve tested:\\n\\n• You shouldn\u0027t do X, because whether you fail or succeed something bad happens.\\n• If it\u0027s been true in the past, it\u0027ll probably be true again.\\n• It\u0027s pointless to do X, because it\u0027s not worth the effort / doomed to fail / will make the problem worse.\\n• The principle/claim X is wrong, because here\u0027s a counterexample.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the argument\u0027s pieces (Conc and Evid) and judge its validity.\\n2. Decide whether you\u0027re going to represent this by diagramming or whether you\u0027re going to boil the reasoning down to a slogan / principle.\\n3. Consider articulating for yourself the number of premises, the strength of each claim, the type of each claim, and the recipe of combining the ingredients.\\n\\nThis is the vocabulary we would typically use for type of claim:\\n\\n• Conditional (keep track of compounds: and\u0027s and or\u0027s in trigger / outcome)\\n• Either/or\\n• Normative (should, ought, good, bad)\\n• Quantified (some, most, all, not all, few)\\n• Comparative (more, taller, smarter, weaker)\\n• Causal (leads to, results in)\\n• Statement of fact (usually that “fulfill” a condition from a conditional statement, thereby showing that the conditional applies to that specific case and something can be logically concluded: All girl scouts sell cookies and Shavon is a girl scout. Jaden has never sold cookies. This combination of one conditional and two facts lets us conclude 1. Shavon has sold cookies, and 2. Jaden isn’t a girl scout.)\\n\\nThis is the vocabulary we would typically use to describe the strength of claims:\\n\\n• Certain (all, must, only, can’t, etc.)\\n• Probable (probably, usually, most, likely, etc.)\\n• Possible (some, can, may, not all, might)\\n\\nPre-phrasing\\n\\nRehearse your principle or abstract recipe, and also ask yourself \\\"Which ingredient would be the easiest to find/confirm?\\\" or \\\"Which ingredient is my best initial reference point?\\\" (f.e. “The conclusion needs to be an either/or claim” or “Let me first see if they\u0027re saying that one thing requires another\\\"). You\u0027re doing this for two reasons:\\n\\n1. You want a must-have you can easily scan for so that you can potentially disqualify a couple answers without having to fully read them.\\n2. There is often an ingredient that is a clear “jumping off point” because without it, you can’t evaluate anything else. For example, if my structure were something like, \\\"If X, then Y or Z. Y is not happening. Thus, either Z is happening or X is not happening,” we can’t evaluate whether the rest of the answer matches until we find a matching either/or conditional.\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\nThe correct answer will be more similar than any of the other answer choices. It will almost always have the same number of premises as the original argument. The conclusion will be the same type of claim and strength of claim. However, it is very\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ncommon for the chronological order of claims to have been shuffled around. It doesn\u0027t matter in which order we get the Conclusion or each Premise, as long as we get them.\\n\\nIf we\u0027re ever not finding any answers that match, we may be looking for something too picky and need to soften our must-have list. If we find two answers that seem to match, then we need to get even pickier and look for some subtle difference between the two and compare it back to the original.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Topic Trap - Often one trap answer seems suspiciously similar to the original in terms of its topic (the correct answer has a similar logical structure but almost invariably is discussing a completely different topic)\\n• Bad Premise Match - There might be an ingredient entirely missing (a lot of wrong answers don\u0027t have the same number of premises as the original), or otherwise it will not quite match up in terms of type of claim or strength of claim.\\n• Bad Conclusion Match - It might be the wrong type of claim, the wrong strength of claim, or it might referring to the wrong thing when it comes to matching the flow of the original.\\n• Validity Mismatch - Most of the original arguments are valid or at least very reasonable. If we then read an answer that is clearly flawed, that\u0027s enough reason to reject it.\\n\\nParallel Flaw\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMost similar, parallels, flawed pattern of reasoning, erroneous reasoning, questionable reasoning\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe flawed pattern of reasoning in the argument is most similar to which of the following?\\nThe questionable pattern of reasoning in which of the following arguments most closely parallels that in the argument?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese will tend to either be Conditional / Quantified flaws, or Famous Flaws. There are 5 or 6 famous flaws that show up a lot on these:\\n\\n• Necessary vs. Sufficient (reading a conditional backwards or in opposite fashion) • Part vs. Whole\\n• Failure to Consider Alternative Explanations\\n• Sampling\\n• Ad Hominem\\n• Unproven vs. Proven False\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nIf there are Quantifier words like \\\"all, some, most, few,\\\" it\u0027s usually a good idea to diagram those arguments, since the correct answer will match that structure. For example:\\n\\n• All A\u0027s are B. All A\u0027s are C. Thus, All B\u0027s are C.\\n• Most A\u0027s are B. Most B\u0027s are C. Thus, Some A\u0027s are C.\\n\\nOther arguments won\u0027t be Famous Flaw or Quantified, in which case we should try to think of an objection and then see if we can make that same objection work with each answer choice (it should work with the correct answer.) For example:\\n\\n• Since no one can be both X and Y, if she\u0027s not X, she must be Y.(Couldn\u0027t she be neither?)\\n• We need to choose between A and B. Since, she prefers B and he prefers A, we should go with B. (Wait, why are you choosing that point of view, rather than the other?)\\n• We can\u0027t prove that X is the culprit, nor can we prove Y is the culprit. Thus, we can\u0027t prove that X or Y is the culprit. (Wait, what if they\u0027re the only two suspects? can\u0027t you say for sure it was one or both of them?)\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Read the argument and try to find some Flaw\\n2. Was there any conditional logic? If so, look for illegal reversals or negations. Consider diagramming the parts.\\n3. Were there any quantifier words like some, most, all? Consider diagramming the recipe.\\n4. If there weren\u0027t conditional or quantified language, could this be committing a Sampling, Circular, Ad Hominem, Part vs. Whole, or Unproven vs. Proven False flaw?\\n5. If nothing has applied so far, how could we ACCEPT the evidence but FIGHT the conclusion? What objection would we make?\\n\\nPre-phrasing\\n\\n• If we identified a famous flaw, we\u0027ll just be going to the answers thinking, f.e., \\\"I need a Sampling flaw, where the sample size is too small\\\".\\n• If we identified a conditional or quantified flaw, then we will probably have a 3 or 4 ingredient recipe of claims (usually 2 premises and 1 conclusion). • For all other cases, we need to think of our objection to the original argument in abstract terms. Subtract the topic and think about the form of the objection.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nAbove all, the correct answer has to replicate the same flaw, or be vulnerable to the same objection. It will usually mimic all the same structural pieces, but there are exceptions to that. The other thing essential is the flaw.\\n\\nIf we\u0027re ever not finding any answers that match, we may be looking for something too\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\npicky and need to soften our must-have list. If we find two answers that seem to match, then we need to get even pickier and look for some subtle difference between the two and compare it back to the original.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Topic Trap - Often one trap answer seems suspiciously similar to the original in terms of its topic (the correct answer has a similar logical structure but almost invariably is discussing a completely different topic)\\n• Bad Premise Match - There might be an ingredient entirely missing (a lot of wrong answers don\u0027t have the same number of premises as the original), or otherwise it will not quite match up in terms of type of claim or strength of claim.\\n• Bad Conclusion Match - It might be the wrong type of claim, the wrong strength of claim, or it might referring to the wrong thing when it comes to matching the flow of the original.\\n• Validity Mismatch - If we read an answer that is valid logic, then that\u0027s enough reason to reject it.\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in Answers\\n\\nThese are the most common type of Principle question. They actually feel way more like Sufficient Assumption than Strengthen. But we classify them under Strengthen because the question stem says \\\"which principle most justifies\\\", not \\\"fully justifies\\\". Nevertheless, come at these mofo\u0027s like they\u0027re Sufficient Assumption, and expect it to be all about pre-phrasing an \\\"IF premise, THEN conclusion\\\" conditional and then reading five answers that are fully of conditional logic trigger words. This is a question type where those with conditional logic skills can find quick eliminations, and those without conditional logic skills can be baited into picking answers that have the right ideas, but in the wrong logical order.\\n\\n• Which of the following principles, if valid, most justifies the argument?\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in Stimulus\\n\\nThese have become more popular over the past twenty tests. They\u0027re pretty awesome! We\u0027re given a Principle, and then an argument is attempting to apply that principle. It will be concluding the right side of the principle, but it will always fail to completely establish the left side of the principle. So, all we need to do is pick an answer that finally makes clear that the left side of the principle has been triggered.\\n\\n• Which of the following, if true, most justifies the application of the principle?\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in Answers\\n\\n1. Find the CONCLUSION and the EVIDENCE.\\n2. Pre-phrase \\\"IF premise, THEN conclusion\\\".\\n3. The correct answer should sound like 1/2 Premise language, 1/2 Conclusion language.\\n4. Try to get rid of answer choices quickly if they don\u0027t have Conclusion language\\n5. Get rid of anything that puts the Conclusion on the left side of arrow.\\n6. If there are competing interests being weighed against each other, the correct answer may rank one thing as more important than another.\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in Stimulus\\n\\n1. Diagram the Principle carefully.\\n2. Verify that the Application is concluding the right side of your rule. Ask yourself whether the Application established the left side of the rule (it didn\u0027t, but in what way did it fall short?)\\n3. Go find an answer that clearly establishes the trigger of the principle is applicable.\\n\\nPrinciple-Conform\\n\\nThere are three varieties of Principle-Conform\\n\\n• The stimulus is a principle (or pair of principles)\\n• The answers are principles\\n• There are no explicit principles, just implied/illustrated ones\\n\\nPrinciples in Stimulus\\n\\nWhen the stimulus is providing an explicit principle (or two), we\u0027re usually being tested on our ability to apply a conditional rule.\\n\\nTo find an answer that Conforms to this principle, we want an answer where the situation described matches the Trigger of the principle, and the judgment concluded matches the Outcome of the principle. (Even when the principles aren\u0027t expressed as conditionals, there will still usually be two things we need to make sure match up).\\n\\nPrinciples in Answers\\n\\nIt\u0027s essentially the same mindset as a Principle-Strengthen question. We read the argument and then anticipate the principle underlying the argument by pre-phrasing \\\"IF evidence, THEN conclusion\\\". We expect the correct answer to Link language from the Evidence to the Conclusion\u0027s language. All that\u0027s different is that because the question stem says conform to the author\u0027s reasoning, we can\u0027t pick an answer that\u0027s overly strong. (That usually doesn\u0027t matter, but occasionally it does)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nImplicit Principles\\n\\nThese feel similar to Parallel reasoning questions, because we\u0027re given an argument in the stimulus, and then each answer choice is its own argument on its own unique topic. We\u0027re looking for an underlying similarity to connect the correct answer to the original argument.\\n\\nOn Parallel questions, those similarities come more in the form of technical / structural similarities (i.e. \\\"Since the original argument had a Most premise and a Some premise, the correct answer\u0027s argument will also have those features\\\").\\n\\nBut with Principle-Conform it\u0027s more about the underlying gist or rule-of-thumb. We don\u0027t try to diagram the arguments. We just want to articulate a general moral to the story or operating principle that\u0027s guiding the author\u0027s thinking, and then we try to find an answer that matches that.\\n\\nRole\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nClaim plays what role, statement figures in which way\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe claim that salad dressing is not legally allowed to be marketed as shampoo plays what role in the argument?\\nThe statement that croutons are the Devil\u0027s handmaiden figures in the argument in which of the following ways?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nLSAC is seeking to make it harder than usual to find the Conclusion, so the Conclusion is only the final claim about 10% of the time. 90% of the time the final claim (or the final 2 or 3 claims) is support.\\n\\nIt\u0027s very common on Role questions to have rebuttal-style arguments in which the author is arguing against something. We also want to be on high alert for Intermediate (Subsidiary) Conclusions. This is the question on LSAT where we are most likely to need to worry about an Intermediate Conclusion.\\n\\nOther times, the Conclusion will be indirectly revealed by Support language (F.A.B.S.) like \\\"for, after all, because, since\\\".\\n\\nSometimes, our best clue about where the conclusion is will be an Opinion Indicator like \\\"probably, apparently, evidently\\\".\\n\\nBeware that there may be Intermediate Conclusions. They will pass the Support test because they will have their own premise. But they are there to support the Main Conclusion.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the conclusion.\\n2. Make sure that it sounds like Author\u0027s Opinion (facts ≠ conclusions), is Supported by at least one explicit claim, and isn\u0027t an Intermediate Conclusion.\\n3. Find the supporting idea(s).\\n4. Now take a look at the question stem and see if it was asking about the conclusion or one of the supporting ideas. If so, head over to the answers. If not, think about whether this claim was something Neutral to the conclusion (like Background info) or something Opposing to the author\u0027s argument (an opponent\u0027s conclusion, an opponent\u0027s premise, or a concession to the opponent the author makes)\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\nDon\u0027t overcomplicate it. Go with one of these:\\n\\n• Main Conclusion\\n• Supports\\n• Opposes\\n• Neutral\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\nThe correct answer doesn\u0027t say anything descriptively wrong. The other four do. (Maybe once in a blue moon, you\u0027d have two \\\"true\\\" answers, where you\u0027d have to ask which one\u0027s better.)\\nThe most common correct answer is Premise or some other way of describing Supporting Idea.\\nEasier Role questions will stick to classic language like Premise and Conclusion, whereas harder Role questions will often switch to less familiar abstract language or synonyms.\\n\\n• Main Conclusion - The claim the author seeks to establish, a generalization supported by evidence, the only conclusion,\\n• Premise - Support, partial support (just means there\u0027s more than one premise), evidence, illustration of, example of, unsupported premise\\n• Intermediate Conclusion - Subsidiary conclusion, supported premise, a conclusion but not the main conclusion, a statement for which some support is provided but is used to support the main conclusion\\n• Opposing Position - The view the argument is designed to discredit, the claim the author attempts to show is false\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Wrong Ingredient - These describe something in the paragraph, but not the claim we were asked about\\n• Too Strong - Some answers that about an idea being required or sufficient for the conclusion\\n• Didn\u0027t Happen - These reference something that never happened in the\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nparagraph\\n• Wrong Conclusion - Lots of wrong answers correctly call the claim a Premise but then say it\u0027s supporting some idea that doesn\u0027t match the argument\u0027s Conclusion\\n\\nStrengthen\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMost strengthens, most supports, argument / reasoning / claim\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?\\nThe reasoning above is most supported by which of the following?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\n• Lots of causal arguments -- A Curious Fact (a correlation, an experiment, a survey, a statistic) followed by a Conclusion that makes a Causal Interpretation of that curious fact.\\n• Lots of comparative arguments -- The author will often assume \\\"Because A and B are similar in this way, they\u0027ll be similar in that way.\\\" Or \\\"because this was true before, it will be true again\\\".\\n• Many predictions / plans / recommendations -- These are also implicitly comparative, because we have to analyze all the potential differences between Present vs. Future, or Before-Plan vs. With-Plan.\\n• Not a lot of conditional logic.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the Conclusion.\\n2. Find the Support.\\n3. Ask yourself if you see any Logic Gaps or can think of anything the author is Assuming.\\n4. Consider how you would attack this argument if you wanted to argue the Anti Conclusion.\\n5. Think about what sort of evidence would make this argument more compelling.\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\nIf we hear something being assumed or sense a term shift or New Term in the conclusion, then we look for an answer connecting those ideas.\\n\\n• For Causal arguments, primarily expect to add plausibility to the author\u0027s hypothesis (No Cause, No Effect data points, more Cause/Effect data points, or something else that explains how the causal connection would work or why it\u0027s a\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nlikely hypothesis), but we also might see correct answers ruling out an alternative explanation for the evidence.\\n• For Comparison arguments, we want to be more convinced that the two things being compared or similar (if the author is assuming they\u0027re comparable) or that the two things being compared are different (if the author is assuming they\u0027re different).\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Do they Weaken or feel like they\u0027re going Wrong Direction?\\n• Do they have No Impact (out of scope)?\\n• Are they Too Weak? (some, sometimes, can, may, not all \u003d one data point)\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\n• Provides a Missing Link in the argument’s internal logic?\\n• Makes two things being compared seem more fair to compare? •\\nRules out an alternate explanation for a curious fact?\\n• Increases the plausibility of the author’s causal story? (#1 way - No Cause, No Effect way)\\n• Makes a survey / sample / study / correlation seem more trustworthy or robust? (rare)\\n\\nWeaken\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMost weakens, most undermines, casts doubt on, fails to consider, ignores\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?\\nWhich of the following, if true, casts the most doubt on Tom\u0027s reasoning?\\nThe argument is most vulnerable to criticism because it fails to consider\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\n• Causal arguments -- A Curious Fact (a correlation, an experiment, a survey, a statistic) followed by a Conclusion that shows the Author\u0027s Causal Interpretation of that fact.\\n• Comparative arguments -- The author will often assume \\\"because A and B are similar in this way, they\u0027ll be similar in that way.\\\" Or \\\"because this was true before, it will be true again\\\".\\n• Predictions - What could be different in the future that would keep this prediction from coming true?\\n• Plan / Solution - How could this fail to achieve the goal?\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the Conclusion.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n2. Find the Support.\\n3. Figure out how to argue the Anti-Conclusion, by framing the argument as\\n\\nGIVEN [this evidence], HOW CAN I ARGU E [the Anti-Conclusion]?\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\n• If we hear a Term Shift, we can look for an answer making those two things seem like they\u0027re not connected.\\n• For Causal arguments, most correct answers suggest an alternate explanation for the Curious Fact in the evidence. Others will make the Author\u0027s Causal Story less plausible, often through a Cause / Effect mismatch (cause was present, but effect was absent / cause was absent, but effect was still present)\\n• For Comparison arguments, we will usually be asking ourselves, \\\"What could be a meaningful difference between these two things?\\\"\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Strengthen or drift in that direction\\n• No Impact - Out of Scope / Irrelevant Distinction / Irrelevant Comparison\\n• Too Weak - Words like some, sometimes, can, may, not all \u003d one data point\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\n• Attack a Missing Link in the argument’s internal logic?\\n• Make two things being compared seem meaningfully different? •\\nProvide an alternate explanation for a curious fact?\\n• Undermine the plausibility of the author’s causal story?\\n• Cheapen the data from a survey / sample / study / correlation? (rare)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReasoning Types\\n\\nCausation\\n\\nBy Question Type\\n\\nSTRENGTHEN / WEAKEN / EVALUATE - Tons of arguments present a Curious Fact in the Evidence and then Conclude something Causal. We end up needing to consider alternate storylines as well as the plausibility of the author\u0027s storyline.\\n\\nFLAW - When authors present a correlation or other Curious Fact and then overconfidently conclude that one thing causes another, we can criticize them for \\\"inferring a cause from a mere correlation\\\" or for \\\"failing to consider alternative explanations\\\". (There is a related trap answer that is never correct: \\\"Mistakes cause for effect\\\". It is okay to say the author has failed to consider that maybe she\u0027s got the causal picture backwards, but we aren\u0027t allowed to definitively say she got it backwards)\\n\\nNECESSARY ASSUMPTION - When the author concludes that \\\"X caused Y\\\", she needs to assume \\\"there is not some other storyline for what caused Y\\\" as well as that \\\"X causing Y is a plausible storyline\\\".\\n\\nMUST BE TRUE / MOST SUPPORTED - Sometimes there are chains of causal connections (X causes Y. Y causes Z.), and so the correct answer will reward us for deriving the idea that \\\"X causes Z\\\" or that \\\"if we had less X, we\u0027d have less Z\\\". There are a lot of questions on Most Supported where the correct answer reinforces a Causal Difference-Maker. If we\u0027re told that X is true, and \\\"because of this / due to this\\\" Y is true, then the correct answer will synthesize/regurgitate that \\\"things like X can cause things like Y\\\". On some Most Supported questions, they\u0027re playing a game of Solve the Causal Mystery, where they lay out a few factors and the correct answer offers the most conservative causal guess about how to explain this situation.\\n\\nPARALLEL FLAW - Not too many of these involve causality, but Parallel Flaw questions will occasionally test the Causal Flaw, where an author is too confident in her specific causal guess, even though other (potentially more likely) explanations are possible.\\n\\nPARADOX - Tons of correct answers will involve us needing some potential causal storyline for how two things could be different (or the same, depending). We\u0027re usually scanning the answers looking for some Causal Difference-Maker that would show why two different animals, or two different groups, or two different time periods, or two different situations end up looking differently.\\n\\nCausal Verbs\\nKeywords: causes, leads to, results in, has the effect of, promotes, inhibits, raises,\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nlowers, deters, encourages, influences, contributes to, is a factor in\\nCausal Difference-Maker Language\\nWhen these words start a sentence, it signifies that there\u0027s a causal connection between the previous sentence and this sentence.\\nKeywords: because of this, due to this, this allows, this leads to, this makes possible\\n\\nOn Most Supported tasks, it is legal to say, \\\"If the Causal Difference-Maker weren\u0027t present, the effect probably wouldn\u0027t have been present (or less of the effect would be present)\\\".\\n\\nCurious Facts\\nThese are almost all going to involve some sort of asymmetric comparison:\\n\\n• Ever since we raised the minimum sentence for burglary, there have been fewer burglaries.\\n• People who know how to play chess tend to be good at math.\\n• Subjects assigned to the group that heard Mozart were less likely than those who didn\u0027t to report being stressed by the newspaper headlines they read. • Knowing French is associated with having higher than average numbers of romantic partners.\\n• Only 20% of the cars on the road are red, but 45% of speeding tickets are given to people who drive red cars.\\n\\nKeywords: correlated with, associated with, more likely to, less likely to, tend to\\n\\nCurious Fact → Author\u0027s Causal Story\\n\\nThere\u0027s a huge swath of arguments in LR that fall into the same bucket:\\n\\n• The author presents some Curious Fact in the evidence. (a statistic, the results of a study, an asymmetric comparison)\\n• The conclusion offers a Hypothesis / Explanation./ Interpretation\\n\\nThere are two different ways to affect a causal argument:\\n\\n1. Propose or undermine Other Ways to explain the Curious Fact\\n2. Support or undermine the Plausibility of the Author\u0027s Story\\n\\nHere\u0027s how correct answers for causal arguments play out on various question types:\\n\\n• WEAKEN - They typically suggest some Other Way to explain the Curious Fact (an alternative explanation). Sometimes they undermine the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Story either by suggesting why it\u0027s an unlikely hypothesis or by providing data points that don\u0027t fit with the author\u0027s cause / effect relationship (Cause without Effect, or Effect without Cause)\\n• STRENGTHEN - They typically increase Plausibility of the Author\u0027s Story, particular by offering data points showing that where the Cause was absent the Effect was absent. Sometimes they rule out some Other Way to explain the\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nCurious Fact.\\n• FLAW - They say that it is a flaw to assume that X causes Y, just because they\u0027re correlated or just because that could be an explanation. Failure to consider alternative explanations for the evidence \u003d Causal Flaw.\\n• NECESSARY ASSUMPTION - They tend to rule out some Other Way, but occasionally they provide a missing link that allows the Author\u0027s Story to be plausible.\\n\\nConditional Logic\\n\\nImportance\\n\\nAlthough this topic might affect less than 1/3 of the test, it\u0027s common for it to affect 1/2 or more of a student\u0027s incorrect answers. Conditional logic is found throughout LR and LG. In LR, there will often be conditional logic in the stimulus as well as the answer choices. In LG, around half of all games have at least one conditional logic rule.\\n\\nMost of us aren\u0027t very good at spotting or dealing with conditional logic. It\u0027s not something we\u0027ve had to use in our pre-LSAT life. It often feels too hard, too mechanical, too mathematical. A lot of LSAT students are very good readers, very good at intuitive holistic thinking, and thus struggle with conditional logic unless they really learn some new skills and switch to those tools whenever conditional logic is on the menu.\\n\\nRelevant Question Types\\n\\nHere\u0027s where conditional logic will typically show up:\\n\\n• Sufficient Assumption / Principle-Strengthen (stimulus and answers)\\n• Apply the Principle (stimulus)\\n• Parallel / Parallel Flaw (stimulus and answers)\\n• Must Be True / Must Be False (stimulus and answers)\\n\\nHere\u0027s where conditional logic will often show up:\\n\\n• Necessary Assumption (stimulus and answers)\\n• Most Strongly Supported (stimulus and answers)\\n• Flaw (if they\u0027re testing the Necessary vs. Sufficient flaw)\\n\\nThroughout these problems, look for keywords indicating conditional logic. Listen for claims with total certainty, or ones mentioning requirements. Jot down the \\\"X → Y\\\" version of these claims, and then also write the contrapositive.\\n\\n• (Put this on the LEFT): if, when, the only, all, each, any, every, no, whenever, whoever, wherever\\n• (Put this on the RIGHT): only, only if, requires, depends on, must, ensures,\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nguarantees, implies, necessitates\\n• (Do an IF-NOT translation: put the attached idea on the left, but negated): unless, without, until, except perhaps\\n• No A\u0027s are B \u003d All A\u0027s are ~B\\n• Required things \u003d Right side of arrow\\n\\nIf you have more than one conditional, check to see if they chain together. You may have to write a contrapositive of one of them to see how they would connect. Conditionals chain together when you have the same idea on opposite sides, or opposite ideas on the same side.\\n\\nSame Idea / Opposite Side\\n\\n• A → B (B is on the right side)\\n• B → C (B is on the left side)\\n• Combined, it yields A → B → C\\n\\nOpposite Idea / Same Side\\n\\n• X → Y (Y is on the right side)\\n• Z → ~Y (~Y is on the right side)\\n• Combined, it yields X → Y → ~Z\\n\\nIf you have conditionals + facts, check to see whether any facts trigger the conditional or its contrapositive. Given a rule like, \\\"All unanimous votes occur under time constraints\\\", it would look like this:\\n\\nUnanimous Vote → Time Constraint\\n~Time Constraint → ~Unanimous Vote\\n\\nThe two triggers are Unanimous Vote and No Time Constraint, so we would examine any of the facts presented and see whether they mentioned there being either a Unanimous Vote or a lack of Time Constraint, which would trigger this rule and allow us to infer the right side.\\n\\nComparison\\n\\nAssumes a Similarity\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Linda loves 90\u0027s Hip-Hop, and she\u0027s really into Bubba\u0027s Nasty 90\u0027s Mix on Spotify. Thus, Jacob will probably be really into Bubba\u0027s Nasty 90\u0027s Mix too. Strengthen by making it seem like Jacob is also into 90\u0027s Hip-Hop. Weaken by making it seem like Jacob isn\u0027t into 90\u0027s Hip-Hop, or isn\u0027t into the stuff that Bubba plays on dat Nasty mix of his.\\n\\nEXAMPLE: LSAT students should never take full-length practice tests in the weeks leading up to taking an official LSAT. After all, taking an LSAT, like running a marathon, is a huge test of endurance, and marathon runners do not practice for their races by running full marathons in the weeks leading up to a race.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like LSAT and marathons are fair to compare.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nWeaken by making it seem like there is something meaningfully different about LSAT taking and marathon running (maybe it\u0027s far easier for the brain to recover from the endurance test of an LSAT than it is for bones and muscles to recover from the endurance test of a marathon).\\n\\nAssumes a Difference\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Paula and Jacoby have both never been able to complete a 720 on the half pipe before. But Paula, unlike Jacoby, will probably be able to nail a 720 by the end of this vacation. After all, she\u0027s currently working with a snowboarding instructor.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Jacoby is not also working with a snowboarding instructor.\\nWeaken by making it seem like Jacoby has done or is doing something that would match or exceed the benefit that Paula is getting from her snowboarding instructor.\\n\\nEXAMPLE: We just got back the results from the focus group on Presley\u0027s ad campaign. They found it, \\\"offensive to older people\\\". Thus, we should go with Marley\u0027s ad campaign instead.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Marley\u0027s ad campaign isn\u0027t offensive to older people.\\nWeaken by making it seem like Marley\u0027s campaign is just as offensive to the elderly, or point out some other detriment it has that would match or exceed the negative of Presley\u0027s ad campaign.\\n\\nPast vs. Present\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Paula and Jacoby have both never been able to complete a 720 on the half pipe before. Thus, by the time this vacation is over, they will have again failed to nail a 720 on the half-pipe.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like the past \u003d present in any other regard that might affect their performance. (they\u0027re in comparable shape, they\u0027re comparably motivated, they\u0027re comparably uninjured, their understanding of snowboarding hasn\u0027t significantly improved).\\nWeaken by making it seem like there\u0027s some important difference between before and now.\\n\\nPresent vs. Future\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Over the past decade, the electorate has gotten increasingly polarized and radicalized by partisan media sources. Thus, ten years from now, we probably won\u0027t even be able to have conversations with people from another political party. Strengthen by making it seem like trends will continue or lean even more in the same direction. Weaken by making it seem like something important is going to change (Kanye wins the Presidency as a unity candidate), or something about the current trend is being overlooked (it\u0027s driven by ~80 year old billionaires who will soon be dead).\\n\\nCurrent World vs. World of Plan / Recommendation\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Jasper has had a hard time connecting with his grandmother. He should start watching more daytime soap operas.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nStrengthen by making it seem like this plan will work / it\u0027s feasible / it\u0027s targets a meaningful issue ... the grandma loves daytime soap operas and would be very enthused to talk about them with Jasper ... Jasper wouldn\u0027t have to sacrifice going to school in order to watch these daytime soaps.\\nWeaken by making it seem like this plan isn\u0027t feasible / people won\u0027t be willing to cooperate as the plan intends / the plan will backfire for some reason ... Jasper only has streaming TV and can\u0027t see satellite TV like daytime soaps .. Grandma only likes discussing soaps with her female friends ... Jasper and his grandma would have very conflicting opinions on what\u0027s going on in these shows, adding strain to their relationship.\\n\\nAnti-Causal\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Ivan and Crosby both updated their Tinder profile, but only Ivan updated his picture with a headshot done by a professional photographer. In the month following their profile updates, they each got around 100 matches. Therefore, Ivan\u0027s paying for that professional photographer was apparently a waste of money.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Ivan and Crosby were similar in any other meaningful regard (how attractive they are, how good/bad their personality description is, how many Matches they were previously getting).\\nWeaken by making it seem like Ivan and Crosby were not fair to compare from the start ... Ivan was starting from a lower reference point, and so achieving an equal number of matches to Crosby is actually showing that the pro photographer did make a difference (Ivan\u0027s less appealing / Crosby already had a pro photo for his pic / Ivan used to only get 40 matches per month whereas Crosby had been getting 80 per month).\\n\\nPercentage vs. Amount\\n\\nEXAMPLE: The average age of students at Hank\u0027s Ballroom Dancing School has decreased in recent years. Evidently, there are more and more young people rediscovering this timeless style of dancing.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like there are at least as many older people as there were before (rule out the possible objection that the average age went down not because younger people joined but because older people left)\\nWeaken by making it seem like the real reason that the average age has gone down is because there aren\u0027t as many older people or find a way to make it seem implausible that ballroom dancing is increasing in popularity among younger people.\\n\\nPros vs. Cons\\n\\nEXAMPLE: People who start smoking cigarettes regularly tend to have less appetite than they did before smoking. So, if you want to be healthier, try starting a cigarette habit so that you can lose some weight!\\nStrengthen by making it seem like the advantages to smoking outweigh the disadvantages.\\nWeaken by making it seem like even though smoking cigs might do one thing that could make you healthier (help you lose weight), it might have some negatives associated with it that would outweigh the advantages.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nEXAMPLE: The Baxter vacuum has more rug settings than The Suckster. Thus, Reggie, who is in the market for a new vacuum, should buy the Baxter rather than the Suckster.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Reggie cares at least somewhat about having lots of rug settings, or by providing other ways in which the Baxter is equal to or better than the Suckster.\\nWeaken by making it seem like Reggie doesn\u0027t care about number of settings, or that most of the extra settings are dumb/useless, or by providing any sense in which the Baxter is worse than the Suckster (price, weight, convenience, etc.)\\n\\nComplete the Analogy\\n\\nLots of examples of Logical Completion (i.e. fill in the blank) LR questions involve pulling together two strands of a conversation to complete a comparison. EXAMPLE: Singing an opera is just like running a marathon. Just as you wouldn\u0027t want to run a marathon without first stretching out your tendons, clearly ______ . Complete the analogy by making it seem like we should \\\"stretch out our tendons\\\" before we sing an opera (limber up our vocal cords?)\\n\\nExplain Curious Fact\\n\\nWhen Causation appears in the Assumption Family, it almost always involves an author speculating a possible Explanation to account for a given Curious Fact. We learn to consider alternate explanations and to evaluate the plausibility of the author\u0027s explanation.\\nThis type of argument is particularly common in Strengthen / Weaken / Evaluate, but it will also show up a fair amount on Flaw and Necessary Assumption.\\n\\nPrephrasing, by Question Type\\n\\nSTRENGTHEN\\n\\nBy ruling out an Alternate Explanation for curious fact\\nOR\\nBy increasing the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Explanation.\\nThe 2nd option is more common on Strengthen, and the most common type of plausibility strengthener is an answer that shows \\\"things not exposed to the cause do not exhibit the effect\\\".\\n\\nWEAKEN\\n\\nBy suggesting an Alternate Explanation for curious fact\\nOR\\nBy lowering the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Explanation.\\nThe 1st option is more common on Weaken.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nEVALUATE\\n\\nThe correct answer choice asks a question whose answer would suggest an Alternate Explanation for the curious fact, or whose answer would establish a baseline of plausibility for the Author\u0027s Explanation. The 1st option is more common.\\n\\nFLAW\\n\\nBy complaining that the author has over confidently assumed one specific causal relationship from evidence that could be interpreted different ways,\\nOR\\nBy complaining that the author has failed to consider an Alternate Explanation for the curious fact\\nOR\\nBy complaining that the author has failed to consider some fact that would hurt the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Explanation.\\nThe 1st option is more common on Flaw (just describing that the author\u0027s conclusion is too aggressively sure of itself, given the evidence).\\n\\nNECESSARY ASSUMPTION\\n\\nBy ruling out an Alternate Explanation for curious fact\\nOR\\nBy establishing some fact that\u0027s necessary for the Author\u0027s Explanation to be plausible.\\nThe 1st option is more common on Necessary Assumption.\\n\\nFind the Curious Fact\\nLook for an asymmetry between two groups or two situations or two timeframes. Look for a correlation or statistical change. Or just look for any funky fact that makes you scratch your head and think, \\\"why?\\\"\\nexamples\\n\\n• People who write poetry are more likely than those who don\u0027t to be in committed relationships\\n• Revenue at our company decreased by 17% last week\\n• This year\u0027s craft fair had fewer macaroni sculptures than before • The purple catfish is known to circle around an area more than 100 times before deciding to lay its eggs there\\n• In a study, a group of people who hate vegetables all had the same gene, XRV20, in common\\n\\nAsk Yourself the Curious Question\\nTurn these facts into questions.\\n\\n• Why is there a correlation between poetry and relationships?\\n• Why did revenue go down 17% last week?\\n• Why are there fewer macaroni sculptures this year? (or, why were there more macaroni sculptures last year?)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n• Why does the purple catfish make 100+ circles before laying its eggs? •\\nHow come all the veggie haters have the same gene in common?\\n\\nSometimes the easiest way to find the Curious Question is to look at the Conclusion and picture the question that it\u0027s answering.\\nCONCLUSION: Apparently, Jim was drinking the night of his accident.\\nThe question must have been, \\\"Why did Jim get into an accident?\\\"\\nCONCLUSION: Scientists hypothesize that the crickets use this wax to fortify their nest.\\nThe question must have been, \\\"How is this wax helpful to crickets?\\\"\\n\\nArticulate the Author\u0027s Causal Storyline (Explanation)\\nMake sure to spell out every step in the causal sequence of the author\u0027s story. They frequently leave many parts of it implied.\\nMost people who scored an eventual 170+ on their LSAT took a practice test on LawHub. Thus, if you want to get into a top law school, you should take a practice test on LawHub.\\nThe causal story here is something like, \\\"You don\u0027t have a 170 yet. A 170+ would be very helpful, if not necessary, to get into a top law school. If you take a practice test on LawHub, it will help you to raise your score to 170+.\\\"\\nPose the Two-Pronged Question\\n\\n1. Is there some other way to explain the Curious Fact?\\n2. How plausible is the author\u0027s way?\\n\\nRemind yourself, based on the question type, whether you want the answer to suggest / rule out an Alternate Explanation, or whether you want the answer to increase / decrease the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Explanation.\\n\\nCategorize the Correct Answers\\nWhen you\u0027re reviewing these questions, the skill you\u0027re trying to develop is the ability to speak this language:\\n\\\"Choice (B) is correct because it rules out an Alternate Explanation for why all the birds were lethargic.\\\"\\n\\nFamous Flaws\\n\\nWhere do we care?\\n\\nThese patterns are primarily of importance when it comes to Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions, although a couple of them (Causal and Nec vs. Suff) represent Reasoning Patterns (Causation and Conditional Logic) that permeate most of the LR section.\\n\\nWhy do we care?\\n\\nFlaw is the most prevalent question type in LR, and around 40% of the answer choices on Flaw refer to one of these ten famous flaws. If you wanted to memorize a handful more, then you\u0027d get around 13-15 types of flaws worth memorizing. The primary value\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nof knowing them will be eliminating wrong answers. Most of the time we see these Famous Flaws in an answer choice, they\u0027ll be wrong. But sometimes, maybe 35% of the time, the actual problem in the Flaw stimulus is one of these Famous Flaws.\\n\\nOn Parallel Flaw, we need to be able to either construct an abstract recipe of what the argument has done or to label it with some sort of Objection. For this latter category, the conversational objection we would think of is usually one of the top 6 Famous Flaws (Nec vs. Suff, Causal, Part vs. Whole, Sampling, Ad Hom, or Unproven vs. Proven False).\\n\\nTell me the Famous Flaws, already!\\n\\nHere is a description and an example of the top 10 Famous Flaws that show up in answer choices, followed by a few more that almost made the cut.\\n\\nNecessary vs. Sufficient - Taking a conditional relationship and then illegally thinking through it in backwards or opposite fashion. This is frequently the actual flaw on Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions.\\n\\n• People who have seen \\\"The 13th\\\" have a great understanding of the US prison system. Since Megan still hasn\u0027t seen that documentary, she must have a poor understanding of the US prison system.\\n• People who have seen \\\"The 13th\\\" have a great understanding of the US prison system. Jane understands the US prison system really well, so clearly she has seen \\\"The 13th\\\".\\n\\nCausal - Overconfidently concluding one possible causal interpretation of a curious fact, usually a Correlation, when other possible interpretations exist.\\n\\n• People who have been enlisted in the military are more likely than those who haven\u0027t to have excellent physiques. Evidently, military recruitment officers select their future soldiers in large part based on the applicant\u0027s fitness and strength.\\n\\nPart vs. Whole - Assuming that a trait that is true of each Part of a collective is therefore true of the Whole, or vice versa.\\n\\n• The military is part of the government, and the military is conducted with lots of honor and discipline. Thus, the government overall is conducted with lots of honor and discipline.\\n• Since rat poison is very harmful if ingested, each ingredient in rat poison would be very harmful if ingested.\\n\\nUnproven vs. Proven False - Pointing out that someone failed to prove X is true, and then concluding that this means that X is false.\\n\\n• Billy says that his uncle was related to Elvis, but he hasn\u0027t been able to connect them on Ancestry.com. Thus, Billy\u0027s uncle was not related to Elvis. •\\nSome claim that the Atlanta Braves should change their name because they are the only major sports team that still has a Native American mascot. However, the\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nChicago Blackhawks still have a Native American mascot. Thus, the Braves should not change their name.\\n\\nAd Hominem - Dismissing a point of view because the source has a biased interest or has conflicting past behavior.\\n\\n• My friend Sameer says that social workers would be better at responding to domestic abuse calls than would armed police officers. But we can ignore this notion, since Sameer is a social worker and so his industry would benefit were cities to make this switch.\\n• My governor is telling me that we should all wear masks now, but we should not heed her words. After all, she was vocally against wearing masks a month ago.\\n\\nSampling - Extrapolating/generalizing too readily from a small sample, while failing to consider that the sample might be biased, too small, or unrepresentative.\\n\\n• Charlie, a staunch Republican, is from San Francisco and works for a theater company. Since Sheila is also from San Francisco and also works for a theater company, she is probably a staunch Republican too.\\n• Today\u0027s youth prefer comic books to literature. After all, in a survey conducted at last year\u0027s Comic-Con, over 85% of the young people surveyed reported liking comic books much more than novels.\\n\\nThere are several things you can do, in complementary fashion, to increase your facility with Famous Flaws:\\n\\n• Flip through a bunch of Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions, mainly scanning the answers on Flaw to see if you recognize any of these famous flaws, or by reading through the arguments to see if any of them seem like they are committing one of these flaws.\\n• Create your own flashcards (potentially reversible ones), where you either see the name of a famous flaw and quiz yourself on what an answer choice describing that flaw would sound like, or vice versa (you read the answer choice and quiz yourself on whether you can name the flaw)\\n• Write your own example set for these flaws. For the flaws that have more than one style, try writing an example for each style. Send them to another LSAT student, or post on Reddit, or email Laura / Matt / Patrick if you want some feedback on them.\\n----------------------------------\\nIrrelevant Rebuttal - Rebutting someone\u0027s claim or argument by mischaracterizing it in some Straw Man way or by bringing up something wholly irrelevant to the original claim.\\n\\n• My neighbor complains about how loud my music is, saying that the bass frequencies rattle all her furniture. But she must be lying, because I only play country music, not rock.\\n\\nFalse Choice - Acting like you have to choose between two options even though you never established those are the only two options.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n• We could use Grandma\u0027s birthday money to buy the new Mario Kart. But we could also use it to buy a rope swing for the yard. Since we can\u0027t find Mario Kart available anywhere online, we\u0027ll have to buy the rope swing.\\n\\nRelative vs. Absolute - Getting mixed up with Percents vs. Amounts, or confusing more/less language with yes/no language.\\n\\n• Some say that older people are never great at video games, but they\u0027re wrong. After all, Grandma is much better than Uncle Toby at Mario Kart\\n\\nInappropriate Appeal - Your evidence is a plea for sympathy, you cite an \\\"authority\\\" without establishing credibility, or you favor one source\u0027s opinion over another source\u0027s, without justifying your pick. Almost always wrong answers:\\n\\n• Elon Musk tweeted that concerns about the impact of 5g radiation are conspiracy theories, so we needn’t worry about renting this house next to a 5g cell tower. (Appeal to Authority)\\n• I know I was speeding, Officer, but you shouldn\u0027t give me a ticket because it would ruin my Anniversary. (Appeal to Emotion)\\n• The majority of voters in Country A don’t believe rising sea levels pose a problem for their country, so we should abandon legislation that would protect the coast against what local climatologists say is a looming threat. (Appeal to Popular Opinion)\\n\\nCircular Reasoning - The premise of an argument restates or assumes the conclusion.\\n\\n• The LSAT is clearly the hardest grad school test, because all the other tests people take to go to grad school pale in comparison to the difficulty of the LSAT.\\n\\nInternal Contradiction - Something the author says undermines or contradicts something else they say\\n\\n• LSAT is an incredibly challenging test for anyone who takes it. However, if you commit to Eddie Spaghetti\u0027s Steady LSAT Readiness protocol, you will soon be feeling like LSAT is no harder than a kindergarten spelling quiz.\\n\\nEquivocation - Using the same \\\"term\\\" or concept to mean two very different things.\\n\\n• Some question whether Egypt condones Romania\u0027s recent military buildup on their eastern border. But clearly Egypt sanctions this activity, since in the weeks following the Romanian military\u0027s actions, Egypt issued sanctions against Romania.\"}",
      "role": "model",
      "finishReason": "STOP",
      "tokenCount": 17811,
      "isJson": true,
      "parts": [{
        "text": "{\"response\":\"once, or that one group must be bigger than another.\\n\\nReading Goal\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n1. As we read facts, we\u0027re looking for two or more ideas that we could synthesize."
      }, {
        "text": " Look for clues about where that synthesis might come, by keeping your eyes peeled for Conditional, Causal, Math-y"
      }, {
        "text": ", or Comparison / Contrast language. In particular, look out for overlapping ideas. An idea that shows up more than once"
      }, {
        "text": " is often the conduit to chaining something together, triggering a rule, or otherwise making an inference.\\n2. If you"
      }, {
        "text": " see a Pivot, think about what it would sound like to safely combines what comes after the pivot with what came before.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n3. If it\u0027s Logical Completion (fill in the blank), think about a safely worded conclusion that would pull"
      }, {
        "text": " together the thoughts already mentioned.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nThe"
      }, {
        "text": " most provable claim there is always the correct answer. Sometimes it\u0027ll feel 99% must be true."
      }, {
        "text": " Other times it will feel more speculative, but it\u0027s always the most supported. It does not need to use all"
      }, {
        "text": " the facts; in fact, it\u0027s a very common template that the inference involves combining the first and last claims ("
      }, {
        "text": "while the middle claim acts as filler).\\n\\nCorrect answers will often have easy-to-prove language like \\\""
      }, {
        "text": "some, could, can, may, might, sometimes, not always, not all, need not\\\".\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nIt\u0027s very rare for the correct answer to be derived from only one idea, so you should normally feel like"
      }, {
        "text": " in order to support the correct answer, you need to combine 2 or more facts.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n• Too Strong - Words like \\\"all, none, only, unless\\\", as well as medium-strong"
      }, {
        "text": " words like \\\"typically, usually, most, generally, tends to, primarily\\\" often are stronger than what the information has"
      }, {
        "text": " told us.\\n• Out of Scope - Answers that bring up things for which we were provided no information are almost always"
      }, {
        "text": " wrong\\n• Illegal Reversals / Negations - If there is any conditional logic involved, there will usually be"
      }, {
        "text": " at least one trap answer that represents the conditional logic in backwards or opposite fashion.\\n• Unknown Comparisons - A lot of"
      }, {
        "text": " correct answers take an absolute adjective like \\\"successful\\\" and try to make a comparative claim like \\\"more successful\\\" out of"
      }, {
        "text": " it.\\n• Contradicted - Some answers actually go against what we read.\\n\\nMust be True\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nKeywords\\n\\nStatements, information, passage, must be true, properly inferred, follows logically\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nExamples\\n\\nIf the statements are true, which one of the following must be true?\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Which of the following can be inferred by the information in the passage?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nThese are FACTS, not ARGUMENTS.\\nThe stimulus usually features some blend of Conditional / Quantified ideas.\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nConditional Inferences\\n\\nWe often have to chain together multiple"
      }, {
        "text": " conditionals.\\nWhen we have a chain of ideas, such as A → B → C → D, the most likely"
      }, {
        "text": " correct answer would be A → D or the contrapositive ~D → ~A. But any subset of that chain can"
      }, {
        "text": " be properly inferred, such as A → C or B → D.\\nThe other common setup is that we have a fact"
      }, {
        "text": " that triggers a conditional rule (usually by contrapositive) and lets us infer the outcome of the rule.\\nWe may"
      }, {
        "text": " have 2 or 3 possible options identified, but through the course of ruling out all but 1 option, we"
      }, {
        "text": " are forced to infer that final option.\\n\\nQuantified / Mathematical Inferences\\n\\nIf we see"
      }, {
        "text": " words like \\\"some, most, all, few\\\", then the correct answer might be testing that two qualities overlap at least"
      }, {
        "text": " once, or that one group must be bigger than another.\\n\\nMost A\u0027s are B ....... Most A"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s are B\\nMost A\u0027s are C ....... Most B\u0027s are ~A\\nSome B"
      }, {
        "text": " are C ........More B\u0027s than A\u0027s\\n\\nAll A\u0027s are B ....... Most A"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s are B\\nSome A\u0027s are C ......All B\u0027s are C\\nSome B are"
      }, {
        "text": " C ....... Most A\u0027s are C\\n\\nThere are other mathematical ideas tested, relating to % change,"
      }, {
        "text": " averages, and inequalities.\\n\\n• If x \u003e y, and y \u003e z, then x \u003e z.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• If the avg score for Group A was 160, and the avg score for the non-teachers"
      }, {
        "text": " in the group was 152, then the avg score for the teachers in the group had to be higher than"
      }, {
        "text": " 160.\\n• If my rent is higher now than five years ago, but my rent represents that same"
      }, {
        "text": " proportion of my salary as it did five years ago, then my salary now is higher than five years ago.\\n\\nReading"
      }, {
        "text": " Goal\\n\\nAs we read facts, we\u0027re looking for two or more ideas that we could synthesize."
      }, {
        "text": " Look for clues about where that synthesis might come, by keeping your eyes peeled for Conditional, Quantified, or otherwise Mathematical"
      }, {
        "text": " ideas.\\n\\nIf you see Conditional language, look for a fact that triggers the rule (or, more likely"
      }, {
        "text": ", that triggers the contrapositive). If you have more than one Conditional, see if they chain together.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "If you see a Pivot holding two mathematical ideas in tension, think about how to mathematically reconcile those two ideas.\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nIf you see words like \\\"Some / Most / All\\\", ask yourself if you have two MOST facts about\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nthe same group. If so, you can infer some overlap to"
      }, {
        "text": " the two qualities mentioned.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nIt is provable based on only the provided information"
      }, {
        "text": ". It does not need to use all the facts; in fact, it\u0027s a very common template that the inference"
      }, {
        "text": " involves combining the first and last claims (while the middle claim acts as filler). It\u0027s very rare for the correct"
      }, {
        "text": " answer to be derived from only one idea, so you should normally feel like in order to support the correct answer, you"
      }, {
        "text": " need to combine 2 or more facts.\\n\\nWe would gravitate more towards answers with easy-to-prove"
      }, {
        "text": " language like \\\"some, could, can, may, might, sometimes, not always, not all, need not\\\"."
      }, {
        "text": " However, on Must Be True it is very common for the correct answer to have strong, black-and-white language"
      }, {
        "text": ". Since almost all the inferences come from Conditional or Mathematical thinking, they have the certainty that justifies strong language.\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Too Strong - Words like all, none, only, unless, as well as"
      }, {
        "text": " medium-strong words like typically, usually, most, generally, tends to, primarily often are stronger than what the information"
      }, {
        "text": " has told us.\\n• Out of Scope - Answers that bring up things for which we were provided no information are almost"
      }, {
        "text": " always wrong\\n• Reversed / Opposite logic - If there is any conditional logic involved, there will usually be at least"
      }, {
        "text": " one trap answer that represents the conditional logic in backwards or opposite fashion.\\n• Unknown Comparisons - A lot of trap answers"
      }, {
        "text": " take an absolute adjective like \\\"successful\\\" and try to make a comparative claim like \\\"more successful\\\" out of it.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• Contradicted - Some answers actually go against what we read.\\n\\nMust be False\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nKeywords\\n\\nMust be false, incompatible, violates, most justifiably rejected\\n\\nExamples\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nIf the statements are true, which one of the following must be false? Based on the statements, each"
      }, {
        "text": " of the following could be true EXCEPT: Which of the following is LEAST COMPATIBLE with the information in the passage"
      }, {
        "text": "? Which of the following situations would violate the principle stated? The passage provides the strongest reason for rejecting which of the following"
      }, {
        "text": "?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese are FACTS, not ARGUMENTS.\\nMust Be False"
      }, {
        "text": " question stems will usually have fact sets that include Conditional or Mathematical ideas.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nMost Strongly Opposed question stems will usually have fact sets that feature a Similarity, Distinction, or Causal Difference"
      }, {
        "text": "-Maker.\\n\\nConditional\\n\\nIf we have more than one conditional, chain them together.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "If we get a conditional and some fact about X applies to the trigger or to the contrapositive\u0027s trigger, then"
      }, {
        "text": " infer the outcome about X.\\nThe correct answer will either contradict a conditional or conditional chain, by providing a case in which"
      }, {
        "text": " the trigger is true but the outcome isn\u0027t, or it will contradict an inference we derived from applying a conditional rule"
      }, {
        "text": " to fact X.\\n\\nMathematical\\n\\nIf we see words like \\\"some, most, all,"
      }, {
        "text": " few\\\", then the correct answer might be testing that two qualities overlap at least once, or that one group must be bigger"
      }, {
        "text": " than another.\\n\\nMost A\u0027s are B ........ Most A\u0027s are B\\nMost A\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s are C ........ Most B\u0027s are ~A\\nSome B are C .........More B\u0027s than A"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s\\n\\nAll A\u0027s are B ....... Most A\u0027s are B\\nSome A\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s are C ....... All B\u0027s are C\\nSome B are C ........ .Most A\u0027s are C"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nThere are other mathematical ideas tested, relating to % change, averages, and inequalities.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• If x \u003e y, and y \u003e z, then x \u003e z.\\n• If the avg score for Group"
      }, {
        "text": " A was 160, and the avg score for the non-teachers in the group was 152"
      }, {
        "text": ", then the avg score for the teachers in the group had to be higher than 160.\\n• If"
      }, {
        "text": " my rent is higher now than five years ago, but my rent represents that same proportion of my salary as it did five"
      }, {
        "text": " years ago, then my salary now is higher than five years ago.\\n\\nA correct answer would simply contradict whatever mathematical"
      }, {
        "text": " inference we\u0027re able to make.\\n\\nCausal Difference-Maker\\n\\nWhen Inference passages give"
      }, {
        "text": " us causal language, it factually establishes a causal influence. Look out for claims that get connected with language like\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n• Because of this\\n• Due to this\\n• This allows\\n• This makes possible\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• By doing this\\n• This leads to\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Resulting in\\n• Contributes to\\\".\\n\\nExample: Patrick started chewing gum during his timed"
      }, {
        "text": " sections. By doing this, his score improved.\\nFrom that setup, it would be Must Be False-ish to"
      }, {
        "text": " say stuff like, \\\"New routines never lead to improvement\\\" or \\\"The activities of your jaw are wholly independent of your"
      }, {
        "text": " performance on a test.\\\"\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\nRead this as though you\u0027re trying to"
      }, {
        "text": " make a valid inference, because they will often contradict a valid inference that can be made. Pay particular attention to whether there"
      }, {
        "text": " is any Conditional language. Can conditionals be chained together or can a rule be applied to a fact to derive a 2"
      }, {
        "text": "nd fact? If there\u0027s not any conditional logic, then there\u0027s likely a mathematical inference that can be made"
      }, {
        "text": " or a causal difference-maker situation discussed.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nIt is CONTRADICTED"
      }, {
        "text": " by the information in the passage (or in Least Compatible / Most Justifiably Rejected, it is badly undermined by something in"
      }, {
        "text": " the passage).\\n\\nIf it\u0027s contradicting a conditional rule, it will establish that the trigger of the rule"
      }, {
        "text": " is happening, but the outcome is not happening. If it\u0027s contradicting an inference we made, it\u0027ll just"
      }, {
        "text": " sound like a negation of that inference or an impossibility, given the truth of that inference.\\n\\nThe correct answer is"
      }, {
        "text": " often strongly worded, because contradicting a strong statement requires something very weak. (if you say \\\"all ice cream is delicious"
      }, {
        "text": "\\\", I can contradict you with just one batch of Cousin Larry\u0027s Meatballs \u0026 Marshmallows ice cream).\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Weak Language - It\u0027s hard to contradict a weak claim, because you"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027d have to prove something really strong. If someone said, \\\"Some cops abuse their power\\\", it would be hard"
      }, {
        "text": " to contradict them, since \\\"some\\\" is so weak and so contradicting it would require arguing that \\\"no cops ever have"
      }, {
        "text": " abused their power\\\".\\n• Out of Scope - Answers that bring up things for which we were provided no information are"
      }, {
        "text": " almost always wrong. We can\u0027t contradict an idea if we never covered that idea.\\n• Reversed / Opposite logic"
      }, {
        "text": " - There are illegal inferences, but that\u0027s not the same thing as a contradiction. Students frequently confuse Must Be False"
      }, {
        "text": " with \\\"this can\u0027t be inferred\\\". Don\u0027t conflate Must Be False with Unsupported. We\u0027re saying"
      }, {
        "text": " there IS support, for the OPPOSITE of this answer.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nNecessary Assumption\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nAssumption, depends, relies, requires, must be assumed,"
      }, {
        "text": " assumes, presupposes, takes for granted\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following is an assumption"
      }, {
        "text": " on which the argument depends?\\nThe author assumes which of the following?\\nThe argument relies on which of the following assumptions?\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nNecessary Assumption is a real mixed bag. Probably 60%"
      }, {
        "text": " of them are testing us on Idea Math, seeing if we notice a missing piece in the author\u0027s logic, similar"
      }, {
        "text": " to what we\u0027re doing on Sufficient Assumption and Principle-Justify questions. The other 40% are really"
      }, {
        "text": " testing us on Possible Objections, seeing if we can think of a way to accept the author\u0027s evidence but reject"
      }, {
        "text": " her conclusion, similar to what we\u0027re doing on Flaw, Strengthen, and Weaken.\\n\\nWhen"
      }, {
        "text": " we see Conditional logic, other chains of ideas, or repeated ideas, (i.e. going to a museum cal"
      }, {
        "text": "ms your soul, and a calm soul finds it easier to think. thus, going to a museum makes you more likely"
      }, {
        "text": " solve a difficult problem), that\u0027s usually a strong sign that the correct answer will simply connect ideas that were only mentioned"
      }, {
        "text": " once (you\u0027re more likely to solve a difficult problem if it\u0027s easier to think).\\n\\nWhen"
      }, {
        "text": " we see arguments that are using Comparison or Causal reasoning structures, then it\u0027s more likely that the correct answer is"
      }, {
        "text": " testing our ability to see where potential objections could arise. When an argument is comparative in nature, the author often needs to"
      }, {
        "text": " assume two things are relevantly similar or needs to assume there\u0027s a meaningful difference between the option they\u0027re choosing"
      }, {
        "text": " and the one they\u0027re rejecting. When an argument is causal in nature (i.e. the author\u0027s"
      }, {
        "text": " conclusion involves one possible causal explanation for some curious background fact), the author needs to assume that her explanation achieves minimal standards of"
      }, {
        "text": " plausibility and, assuming the author\u0027s conclusion sounds pretty sure of itself, the author needs to assume that there is"
      }, {
        "text": " not some other way to explain the background fact.\\n\\nIf an author is rebutting someone\u0027s position,"
      }, {
        "text": " she needs to assume that the reason she offers for her rebuttal actually conflicts with the position she\u0027s trying to reject."
      }, {
        "text": " If an author is rejecting a conditional, she needs to provide a situation where the trigger occurred, but the outcome did not"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nNegating Answers\\n\\nOne of the most"
      }, {
        "text": " reliable ways to get Necessary Assumption answers correct is to get good at the Negation Test: the correct answer, if negated"
      }, {
        "text": ", badly weakens the argument. An incorrect answer, if negated, doesn\u0027t weaken the argument. Negating a claim"
      }, {
        "text": " means contradicting it in the most minimal fashion. The negation of \\\"All my friends are honest\\\" isn\u0027t \\\"None"
      }, {
        "text": " of my friends are honest\\\", it\u0027s \\\"at least one of my friends is not honest\\\". There are a few"
      }, {
        "text": " standards to go by when negating:\\n\\n• Don\u0027t negate every single answer as you read them."
      }, {
        "text": " Try to get rid of some for easier reasons like Out of Scope or Too Strong wording. Use the negation test more to"
      }, {
        "text": " confirm the answer you\u0027re picking.\\n• If the truth value of an answer hinges on a quantity word, f"
      }, {
        "text": ".e. \\\"Some clowns are scary\\\", then negate the quantity term. Know your quantity yin-yangs (Some"
      }, {
        "text": " vs. None / All vs. Not all / Most vs. Not-Most, i.e. 51"
      }, {
        "text": "% vs. 49% / Many vs. Not-Many / Sometimes vs. Never)\\n• If the"
      }, {
        "text": " truth value hinges on the word not, then just remove the not, f.e. negating \\\"Taking the vacation"
      }, {
        "text": " will not deplete Luke\u0027s savings\\\" gives us \\\"taking the vacation will deplete his savings\\\".\\n• If the"
      }, {
        "text": " truth value hinges on the verb, negate the verb, f.e. negating \\\"Washing dishes dries out your"
      }, {
        "text": " hands\\\" gives us \\\"Washing dishes does not dry out your hands\\\".\\n• If the answer is conditional,"
      }, {
        "text": " don\u0027t negate it. Negating a conditional does not give you a conditional. If gives you one counterexample,"
      }, {
        "text": " in which the trigger happens but the outcome does not. Instead of negating conditional answers, just mentally or physically diagram the"
      }, {
        "text": " If / Then statement and ask yourself if the author\u0027s argument made that move. Most of these will be wrong because"
      }, {
        "text": " they\u0027ll be Backwards or Opposite logic.\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the Conclusion"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n2. Find the Support.\\n3. Ask yourself if there\u0027s a New Term or idea in the"
      }, {
        "text": " Conclusion. (If so, that idea will almost always be in the correct answer)\\n4. If there is any"
      }, {
        "text": " conditional logic, or chain of ideas, or repeated ideas, then this problem is probably testing a missing link or fact."
      }, {
        "text": " If there is conditional logic, mentally or physically diagram it. Link conditionals together where possible. Think about the missing conditional piece"
      }, {
        "text": " you would need to get from the Subject of the conclusion (Eddie) to the Predicate (is a great friend).\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n5. If there isn\u0027t conditional logic, switch into debate mode: GIVEN the evidence, HOW COULD YOU"
      }, {
        "text": " ARGUE the anti-conclusion?\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\nBecause of the mixed bag nature"
      }, {
        "text": " of Necessary Assumption arguments, we\u0027ll find that sometimes we have a very specific idea we\u0027re looking for (need"
      }, {
        "text": " to join \u0027easier to think\u0027 with \u0027solve difficult problem\u0027). Other times we\u0027ll have a functional goal for"
      }, {
        "text": " the answer, but not specific wording. If an author is relying on a comparison where two things are\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nsaid to be similar, we might think, \\\"Let\u0027s look for"
      }, {
        "text": " an answer that rules out a significant difference between the two things\\\". If an author is concluding that X is the explanation for"
      }, {
        "text": " some Curious Fact, we might think, \\\"Let\u0027s look for an answer that rules out an alternate explanation or that"
      }, {
        "text": " provides some fact that must be true for X to be a plausible explanation\\\".\\n\\nNecessary Assumptions are the opposite"
      }, {
        "text": " of Objections. If we would object to an author, \\\"hey, maybe those two people are twins!\\\", then"
      }, {
        "text": " we can say the argument was assuming \\\"those two people are not twins\\\". If we could tell an author was assuming \\\""
      }, {
        "text": "Jack likes cookies\\\", then we could object to that argument by saying \\\"Jack doesn\u0027t like cookies\\\". So in some"
      }, {
        "text": " cases, we read a Necessary Assumption paragraph and can already hear something the author is assuming. In those cases, that\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s our pre-phrase. Other times, we don\u0027t hear an assumption, so we switch into debating mode and"
      }, {
        "text": " try to think of a possible objection. If we come up with Objection X, then we can pre-phrase, \\\""
      }, {
        "text": "Okay, the author is assuming that Objection X is not true.\\\"\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\nThere"
      }, {
        "text": " are a few different ways to think about what makes a Necessary Assumption answer correct. They\u0027re all valid in all cases"
      }, {
        "text": ", but you may find that it\u0027s easier to process a correct answer using one formulation vs. another, depending on"
      }, {
        "text": " the problem:\\n\\n• Which answer, when negated, most weakens the argument?\\n• Which answer can clearly"
      }, {
        "text": " be derived from the author\u0027s thinking?\\n• Which answer shows a move that the author clearly made?\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nThe first one is well suited to answers with the word \\\"not\\\" in them, that are ruling out Objections."
      }, {
        "text": " The second one is well suited to answers that are patching together language (like \\\"easier to think\\\" is relevant to"
      }, {
        "text": " \\\"solve difficult problem\\\"), or to answers that assume a relevant similarity or that assume a basic plausibility to an author"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s hypothesis. The third one is well suited to conditional logic answer choices.\\n\\nWeak language is our friend"
      }, {
        "text": ". Correct answers frequently have watered down phrasings like \\\"some, sometimes, can, may, might, not all"
      }, {
        "text": ", not always\\\". Ruling-out language is also our friend. Many correct answers use the word not, next to the"
      }, {
        "text": " verb, to rule out some fact.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Too Strong / Specific - We"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027re accusing the author of assuming the answer we pick, so we don\u0027t want to accuse them of something harsh"
      }, {
        "text": " or crazy sounding (unless they sounded that crazy, and they usually don\u0027t). Words that imply certainty are conditional,"
      }, {
        "text": " so we can evaluate those answers by comparing their conditional logic to the reasoning flow of the argument. But much of the time"
      }, {
        "text": " we can just hear that an idea is way too strong for the author to have committed herself to defending. Just like in"
      }, {
        "text": " Most Supported and Reading Comp, words like most, typically, usually, tends to, generally, primarily, rarely are usually wrong.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Out of Scope - We\u0027re not speculating on what else the author might believe. If we didn\u0027t talk"
      }, {
        "text": " about it, then the author hasn\u0027t made any assumptions about it. (The one exception being that in causal arguments"
      }, {
        "text": " the author will be assuming\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nthat some other explanation that we"
      }, {
        "text": " didn\u0027t talk about is not the actual causal explanation)\\n• Opposite / Backwards Logic - Lots of wrong answers"
      }, {
        "text": " are conditional logic relationships that don\u0027t go from Prem to Conclusion; they go from Conclusion to Premise, or they"
      }, {
        "text": " assume that Not-Premise leads to Not-Conclusion. There are lots of Fake Opposite answers that also just flip a"
      }, {
        "text": " premise. If an author said that \\\"vinegar-based salad dressings are low in fat\\\", a trap answer will say"
      }, {
        "text": " he\u0027s assuming that \\\"non-vinegar-based salad dressings are high in fat\\\". We can\u0027t do"
      }, {
        "text": " that.\\n• Weakens - Some wrong answers are actually objections. The correct answer, when negated, should weaken."
      }, {
        "text": " But an answer, as stated, should never weaken.\\n\\nParadox\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nExplain, resolve, paradox, discrepancy, apparent conflict\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following"
      }, {
        "text": ", if true, most helps to explain the data from the study?\\nWhich of the following, if true, most"
      }, {
        "text": " helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThe paradox usually hinges around Causality"
      }, {
        "text": " and Comparisons.\\nWe are usually needing to figure out the Causal Difference-Maker between two things, two groups,"
      }, {
        "text": " two situations, two timeframes.\\n\\n• Given this sameness, how are they different?\\n• Given"
      }, {
        "text": " this difference, how are they the same?\\n• Given this positive, how is this seen as a negative?\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " Given this negative, how is this seen as a positive?\\n• Given this plan, how come its objective wasn\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "t achieved?\\n• Given X\u0027s alibi, how can we explain why Y happened?\\n\\nSome"
      }, {
        "text": " Explain questions feel a little different. They don\u0027t necessarily pit two things in tension with each other. They just present"
      }, {
        "text": " something and ask us to Explain it. But these are 15% or less of Paradox questions.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "On Explain question stems, you\u0027ll always see a specific reference to what we\u0027re trying to explain. If it"
      }, {
        "text": " says, \\\"Which of the following, if true, most helps to explain Sarah\u0027s reaction to the gift\\\", then"
      }, {
        "text": " we already know that everything else is background, and our job is simply to pick an answer that makes sense of Sarah\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s reaction.\\n\\nOn Resolve question stems, they\u0027re more likely to just use a generic term like \\\""
      }, {
        "text": "paradox, apparent conflict, discrepancy\\\", so then it\u0027s more on us to figure out where the tension is ("
      }, {
        "text": "although half of the tension will always be the final claim).\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Reading Goal\\n\\n1. Understand the paradox. These are not arguments, so do not look for a Prem"
      }, {
        "text": "ise or Conclusion.\\n2. Find the Surprising Claim. It\u0027s almost always the final idea, and it"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s almost always prefaced by a pivot word like However / Yet / Nevertheless.\\n3. What Background Fact("
      }, {
        "text": "s) makes that a surprise? Ask yourself, \\\"Why would we have expected something different from this last, surprising claim"
      }, {
        "text": "?\\\"\\n\\nFrame the tension\\n\\nGIVEN the background claim(s), HOW CAN I EXPLAIN"
      }, {
        "text": " the surprising claim?\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\nIt gives us some way to make sense of the surprising claim or unexpected difference"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n\\nThe stronger the wording, the more impact an answer potentially has, so this is a question type where we"
      }, {
        "text": " Prefer Stronger Language, and where we could consider Weak Language \u003d Red Flag (sometimes, can, may, might,"
      }, {
        "text": " not all, not always).\\n\\nWhenever a question stem says, \\\"Which of the following, if true,"
      }, {
        "text": " most... \\\", (Paradox, Strengthen, and Weaken), a few things apply:\\n\\n• We"
      }, {
        "text": " expect brand new things we haven\u0027t heard before\\n• We prefer stronger language over weaker language\\n• We"
      }, {
        "text": " will have to use a little bit of outside knowledge / common sense to connect what the answer is saying to the impact it"
      }, {
        "text": " has on the stimulus\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Opposite - These actually increase the surprise\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Explains Background - These tell us more about why the background fact is true, but they don\u0027t help us"
      }, {
        "text": " understand why the surprising claim is true\\n• No Distinction - This is special trap answer that we see on Paradox;"
      }, {
        "text": " since we are almost always looking for some meaningful difference between two things / two groups / two timeframes / two situations,"
      }, {
        "text": " if an answer provides something that would be true for both things, for both situations, then it\u0027s useless to us"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n• Too Weak - It\u0027s possible for an answer to lose out to the correct answer because it\u0027s"
      }, {
        "text": " worded much more weakly.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nParallel\\n\\nKeywords\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nMost similar, parallels, pattern of reasoning, structure of reasoning\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe"
      }, {
        "text": " pattern of reasoning in the argument is most similar to which of the following?\\nThe pattern of reasoning in which of the following"
      }, {
        "text": " arguments most closely parallels that in the argument?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese will (almost"
      }, {
        "text": " always) be valid, or at least highly reasonable arguments. We don\u0027t need to push back on the argument when"
      }, {
        "text": " we evaluate it, although recognizing that an original argument is 100% valid can make it that much easier to get rid of"
      }, {
        "text": " any answer choice whose argument is flawed.\\n\\nSince the LSAT is testing patterns of structure, the arguments need"
      }, {
        "text": " a lot of Conditional or Quantified signposts, so that we can successfully represent them abstractly. About 70"
      }, {
        "text": "-75% of these arguments will contain Conditional Logic. The other 25-30% of them can"
      }, {
        "text": " be abstractly captured by some principle like, “if it ain’t broke, don’t fix it\\\", or"
      }, {
        "text": " by some familiar LSAT reasoning pattern like \\\"Just because two things are correlated, doesn\u0027t mean that one causes another"
      }, {
        "text": ", because they could both be effects of some third thing\\\".\\n\\nHere are some of the more common argument"
      }, {
        "text": " templates.\\n\\n• All A\u0027s are B. All B\u0027s are C. X is an A"
      }, {
        "text": ". Thus, X is a C.\\n• If X, then Y or Z. X happened. Y didn\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "t happen. Thus, Z happened.\\n• X is A or B. A doesn\u0027t seem possible. Thus"
      }, {
        "text": ", X is probably B.\\n• Most A\u0027s are B. Most A\u0027s are C. Thus,"
      }, {
        "text": " Some B’s are C’s.\\n• If X, then Y. But Y isn\u0027t true /"
      }, {
        "text": " isn\u0027t good. So, not X / shouldn\u0027t do X.\\n\\nHere are some of the"
      }, {
        "text": " principles / patterns they\u0027ve tested:\\n\\n• You shouldn\u0027t do X, because whether you fail or"
      }, {
        "text": " succeed something bad happens.\\n• If it\u0027s been true in the past, it\u0027ll probably be true again"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n• It\u0027s pointless to do X, because it\u0027s not worth the effort / doomed to fail /"
      }, {
        "text": " will make the problem worse.\\n• The principle/claim X is wrong, because here\u0027s a counterexample.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the argument\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s pieces (Conc and Evid) and judge its validity.\\n2. Decide whether you\u0027re going to represent"
      }, {
        "text": " this by diagramming or whether you\u0027re going to boil the reasoning down to a slogan / principle.\\n3."
      }, {
        "text": " Consider articulating for yourself the number of premises, the strength of each claim, the type of each claim, and the recipe"
      }, {
        "text": " of combining the ingredients.\\n\\nThis is the vocabulary we would typically use for type of claim:\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Conditional (keep track of compounds: and\u0027s and or\u0027s in trigger / outcome)\\n• Either/"
      }, {
        "text": "or\\n• Normative (should, ought, good, bad)\\n• Quantified (some, most,"
      }, {
        "text": " all, not all, few)\\n• Comparative (more, taller, smarter, weaker)\\n• Causal ("
      }, {
        "text": "leads to, results in)\\n• Statement of fact (usually that “fulfill” a condition from a conditional statement,"
      }, {
        "text": " thereby showing that the conditional applies to that specific case and something can be logically concluded: All girl scouts sell cookies and Sh"
      }, {
        "text": "avon is a girl scout. Jaden has never sold cookies. This combination of one conditional and two facts lets us conclude"
      }, {
        "text": " 1. Shavon has sold cookies, and 2. Jaden isn’t a girl scout.)\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nThis is the vocabulary we would typically use to describe the strength of claims:\\n\\n• Certain (all"
      }, {
        "text": ", must, only, can’t, etc.)\\n• Probable (probably, usually, most, likely"
      }, {
        "text": ", etc.)\\n• Possible (some, can, may, not all, might)\\n\\nPre-"
      }, {
        "text": "phrasing\\n\\nRehearse your principle or abstract recipe, and also ask yourself \\\"Which ingredient would"
      }, {
        "text": " be the easiest to find/confirm?\\\" or \\\"Which ingredient is my best initial reference point?\\\" (f."
      }, {
        "text": "e. “The conclusion needs to be an either/or claim” or “Let me first see if they\u0027re"
      }, {
        "text": " saying that one thing requires another\\\"). You\u0027re doing this for two reasons:\\n\\n1. You want"
      }, {
        "text": " a must-have you can easily scan for so that you can potentially disqualify a couple answers without having to fully read them"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n2. There is often an ingredient that is a clear “jumping off point” because without it, you can"
      }, {
        "text": "’t evaluate anything else. For example, if my structure were something like, \\\"If X, then Y or Z"
      }, {
        "text": ". Y is not happening. Thus, either Z is happening or X is not happening,” we can’t evaluate whether"
      }, {
        "text": " the rest of the answer matches until we find a matching either/or conditional.\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nThe correct answer will be more similar than any of the other answer choices. It will almost always have the same number of"
      }, {
        "text": " premises as the original argument. The conclusion will be the same type of claim and strength of claim. However, it is"
      }, {
        "text": " very\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ncommon for the chronological order of claims to have been"
      }, {
        "text": " shuffled around. It doesn\u0027t matter in which order we get the Conclusion or each Premise, as long as we"
      }, {
        "text": " get them.\\n\\nIf we\u0027re ever not finding any answers that match, we may be looking for something"
      }, {
        "text": " too picky and need to soften our must-have list. If we find two answers that seem to match, then we"
      }, {
        "text": " need to get even pickier and look for some subtle difference between the two and compare it back to the original.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Topic Trap - Often one trap answer seems suspiciously similar to the original in terms"
      }, {
        "text": " of its topic (the correct answer has a similar logical structure but almost invariably is discussing a completely different topic)\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " Bad Premise Match - There might be an ingredient entirely missing (a lot of wrong answers don\u0027t have the same"
      }, {
        "text": " number of premises as the original), or otherwise it will not quite match up in terms of type of claim or strength of"
      }, {
        "text": " claim.\\n• Bad Conclusion Match - It might be the wrong type of claim, the wrong strength of claim, or"
      }, {
        "text": " it might referring to the wrong thing when it comes to matching the flow of the original.\\n• Validity Mismatch -"
      }, {
        "text": " Most of the original arguments are valid or at least very reasonable. If we then read an answer that is clearly flawed,"
      }, {
        "text": " that\u0027s enough reason to reject it.\\n\\nParallel Flaw\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Most similar, parallels, flawed pattern of reasoning, erroneous reasoning, questionable reasoning\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe flawed pattern of reasoning in"
      }, {
        "text": " the argument is most similar to which of the following?\\nThe questionable pattern of reasoning in which of the following arguments most closely"
      }, {
        "text": " parallels that in the argument?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\nThese will tend to either be Conditional"
      }, {
        "text": " / Quantified flaws, or Famous Flaws. There are 5 or 6 famous flaws that show up a lot"
      }, {
        "text": " on these:\\n\\n• Necessary vs. Sufficient (reading a conditional backwards or in opposite fashion) • Part vs"
      }, {
        "text": ". Whole\\n• Failure to Consider Alternative Explanations\\n• Sampling\\n• Ad Hominem\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• Unproven vs. Proven False\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nIf there are Quantifier"
      }, {
        "text": " words like \\\"all, some, most, few,\\\" it\u0027s usually a good idea to diagram those arguments, since"
      }, {
        "text": " the correct answer will match that structure. For example:\\n\\n• All A\u0027s are B. All A"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s are C. Thus, All B\u0027s are C.\\n• Most A\u0027s are B. Most"
      }, {
        "text": " B\u0027s are C. Thus, Some A\u0027s are C.\\n\\nOther arguments won\u0027t be"
      }, {
        "text": " Famous Flaw or Quantified, in which case we should try to think of an objection and then see if we can"
      }, {
        "text": " make that same objection work with each answer choice (it should work with the correct answer.) For example:\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Since no one can be both X and Y, if she\u0027s not X, she must be Y.(Couldn"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027t she be neither?)\\n• We need to choose between A and B. Since, she prefers B and"
      }, {
        "text": " he prefers A, we should go with B. (Wait, why are you choosing that point of view, rather than"
      }, {
        "text": " the other?)\\n• We can\u0027t prove that X is the culprit, nor can we prove Y is the"
      }, {
        "text": " culprit. Thus, we can\u0027t prove that X or Y is the culprit. (Wait, what if they\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "re the only two suspects? can\u0027t you say for sure it was one or both of them?)\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Read the argument and try to find some Flaw\\n2. Was"
      }, {
        "text": " there any conditional logic? If so, look for illegal reversals or negations. Consider diagramming the parts.\\n3"
      }, {
        "text": ". Were there any quantifier words like some, most, all? Consider diagramming the recipe.\\n4. If there"
      }, {
        "text": " weren\u0027t conditional or quantified language, could this be committing a Sampling, Circular, Ad Hominem, Part vs"
      }, {
        "text": ". Whole, or Unproven vs. Proven False flaw?\\n5. If nothing has applied so far, how could"
      }, {
        "text": " we ACCEPT the evidence but FIGHT the conclusion? What objection would we make?\\n\\nPre-phrasing\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n• If we identified a famous flaw, we\u0027ll just be going to the answers thinking, f."
      }, {
        "text": "e., \\\"I need a Sampling flaw, where the sample size is too small\\\".\\n• If we identified a"
      }, {
        "text": " conditional or quantified flaw, then we will probably have a 3 or 4 ingredient recipe of claims (usually 2"
      }, {
        "text": " premises and 1 conclusion). • For all other cases, we need to think of our objection to the original argument in"
      }, {
        "text": " abstract terms. Subtract the topic and think about the form of the objection.\\n\\nCorrect Answer\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Above all, the correct answer has to replicate the same flaw, or be vulnerable to the same objection. It will usually"
      }, {
        "text": " mimic all the same structural pieces, but there are exceptions to that. The other thing essential is the flaw.\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nIf we\u0027re ever not finding any answers that match, we may be looking for something too\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\npicky and need to soften our must-have list. If we find two"
      }, {
        "text": " answers that seem to match, then we need to get even pickier and look for some subtle difference between the two and"
      }, {
        "text": " compare it back to the original.\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Topic Trap - Often one trap answer"
      }, {
        "text": " seems suspiciously similar to the original in terms of its topic (the correct answer has a similar logical structure but almost invariably is"
      }, {
        "text": " discussing a completely different topic)\\n• Bad Premise Match - There might be an ingredient entirely missing (a lot of"
      }, {
        "text": " wrong answers don\u0027t have the same number of premises as the original), or otherwise it will not quite match up in"
      }, {
        "text": " terms of type of claim or strength of claim.\\n• Bad Conclusion Match - It might be the wrong type of claim"
      }, {
        "text": ", the wrong strength of claim, or it might referring to the wrong thing when it comes to matching the flow of the"
      }, {
        "text": " original.\\n• Validity Mismatch - If we read an answer that is valid logic, then that\u0027s enough reason"
      }, {
        "text": " to reject it.\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in"
      }, {
        "text": " Answers\\n\\nThese are the most common type of Principle question. They actually feel way more like Sufficient Assumption than"
      }, {
        "text": " Strengthen. But we classify them under Strengthen because the question stem says \\\"which principle most justifies\\\", not \\\"fully justifies\\\"."
      }, {
        "text": " Nevertheless, come at these mofo\u0027s like they\u0027re Sufficient Assumption, and expect it to be all about pre"
      }, {
        "text": "-phrasing an \\\"IF premise, THEN conclusion\\\" conditional and then reading five answers that are fully of conditional logic"
      }, {
        "text": " trigger words. This is a question type where those with conditional logic skills can find quick eliminations, and those without conditional"
      }, {
        "text": " logic skills can be baited into picking answers that have the right ideas, but in the wrong logical order.\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• Which of the following principles, if valid, most justifies the argument?\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen"
      }, {
        "text": " + Principles in Stimulus\\n\\nThese have become more popular over the past twenty tests. They\u0027re pretty"
      }, {
        "text": " awesome! We\u0027re given a Principle, and then an argument is attempting to apply that principle. It will be concluding"
      }, {
        "text": " the right side of the principle, but it will always fail to completely establish the left side of the principle. So,"
      }, {
        "text": " all we need to do is pick an answer that finally makes clear that the left side of the principle has been triggered.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n• Which of the following, if true, most justifies the application of the principle?\\n\\n----------------"
      }, {
        "text": "----------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in Answers\\n\\n1. Find"
      }, {
        "text": " the CONCLUSION and the EVIDENCE.\\n2. Pre-phrase \\\"IF premise, THEN conclusion\\\".\\n3. The"
      }, {
        "text": " correct answer should sound like 1/2 Premise language, 1/2 Conclusion language.\\n4. Try"
      }, {
        "text": " to get rid of answer choices quickly if they don\u0027t have Conclusion language\\n5. Get rid of anything that"
      }, {
        "text": " puts the Conclusion on the left side of arrow.\\n6. If there are competing interests being weighed against each other,"
      }, {
        "text": " the correct answer may rank one thing as more important than another.\\n\\nPrinciple-Strengthen + Principles in"
      }, {
        "text": " Stimulus\\n\\n1. Diagram the Principle carefully.\\n2. Verify that the Application is concluding the right"
      }, {
        "text": " side of your rule. Ask yourself whether the Application established the left side of the rule (it didn\u0027t, but"
      }, {
        "text": " in what way did it fall short?)\\n3. Go find an answer that clearly establishes the trigger of the principle"
      }, {
        "text": " is applicable.\\n\\nPrinciple-Conform\\n\\nThere are three varieties of Principle-Conform"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\n• The stimulus is a principle (or pair of principles)\\n• The answers are principles\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• There are no explicit principles, just implied/illustrated ones\\n\\nPrinciples in Stimulus\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "When the stimulus is providing an explicit principle (or two), we\u0027re usually being tested on our ability to apply a"
      }, {
        "text": " conditional rule.\\n\\nTo find an answer that Conforms to this principle, we want an answer where the situation"
      }, {
        "text": " described matches the Trigger of the principle, and the judgment concluded matches the Outcome of the principle. (Even when the principles"
      }, {
        "text": " aren\u0027t expressed as conditionals, there will still usually be two things we need to make sure match up).\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nPrinciples in Answers\\n\\nIt\u0027s essentially the same mindset as a Principle-Strengthen question. We"
      }, {
        "text": " read the argument and then anticipate the principle underlying the argument by pre-phrasing \\\"IF evidence, THEN conclusion\\\"."
      }, {
        "text": " We expect the correct answer to Link language from the Evidence to the Conclusion\u0027s language. All that\u0027s different is"
      }, {
        "text": " that because the question stem says conform to the author\u0027s reasoning, we can\u0027t pick an answer that\u0027s"
      }, {
        "text": " overly strong. (That usually doesn\u0027t matter, but occasionally it does)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nImplicit Principles\\n\\nThese feel similar to Parallel reasoning questions, because we\u0027re given an argument in the"
      }, {
        "text": " stimulus, and then each answer choice is its own argument on its own unique topic. We\u0027re looking for an underlying"
      }, {
        "text": " similarity to connect the correct answer to the original argument.\\n\\nOn Parallel questions, those similarities come more in the"
      }, {
        "text": " form of technical / structural similarities (i.e. \\\"Since the original argument had a Most premise and a Some premise"
      }, {
        "text": ", the correct answer\u0027s argument will also have those features\\\").\\n\\nBut with Principle-Conform"
      }, {
        "text": " it\u0027s more about the underlying gist or rule-of-thumb. We don\u0027t try to diagram the arguments"
      }, {
        "text": ". We just want to articulate a general moral to the story or operating principle that\u0027s guiding the author\u0027s thinking"
      }, {
        "text": ", and then we try to find an answer that matches that.\\n\\nRole\\n\\nKeywords\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nClaim plays what role, statement figures in which way\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nThe claim that salad"
      }, {
        "text": " dressing is not legally allowed to be marketed as shampoo plays what role in the argument?\\nThe statement that croutons are the"
      }, {
        "text": " Devil\u0027s handmaiden figures in the argument in which of the following ways?\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nLSAC is seeking to make it harder than usual to find the Conclusion, so the Conclusion is only"
      }, {
        "text": " the final claim about 10% of the time. 90% of the time the final claim (or the final 2"
      }, {
        "text": " or 3 claims) is support.\\n\\nIt\u0027s very common on Role questions to have rebuttal-style"
      }, {
        "text": " arguments in which the author is arguing against something. We also want to be on high alert for Intermediate (Subsidiary"
      }, {
        "text": ") Conclusions. This is the question on LSAT where we are most likely to need to worry about an Intermediate Conclusion.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nOther times, the Conclusion will be indirectly revealed by Support language (F.A.B.S.)"
      }, {
        "text": " like \\\"for, after all, because, since\\\".\\n\\nSometimes, our best clue about where the conclusion"
      }, {
        "text": " is will be an Opinion Indicator like \\\"probably, apparently, evidently\\\".\\n\\nBeware that there may be Intermediate"
      }, {
        "text": " Conclusions. They will pass the Support test because they will have their own premise. But they are there to support the Main"
      }, {
        "text": " Conclusion.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the"
      }, {
        "text": " conclusion.\\n2. Make sure that it sounds like Author\u0027s Opinion (facts ≠ conclusions), is Supported by at"
      }, {
        "text": " least one explicit claim, and isn\u0027t an Intermediate Conclusion.\\n3. Find the supporting idea(s).\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "4. Now take a look at the question stem and see if it was asking about the conclusion or one of the supporting"
      }, {
        "text": " ideas. If so, head over to the answers. If not, think about whether this claim was something Neutral to the"
      }, {
        "text": " conclusion (like Background info) or something Opposing to the author\u0027s argument (an opponent\u0027s conclusion, an"
      }, {
        "text": " opponent\u0027s premise, or a concession to the opponent the author makes)\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nDon\u0027t overcomplicate it. Go with one of these:\\n\\n• Main Conclusion\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Supports\\n• Opposes\\n• Neutral\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\nThe correct answer doesn"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027t say anything descriptively wrong. The other four do. (Maybe once in a blue moon, you\u0027d"
      }, {
        "text": " have two \\\"true\\\" answers, where you\u0027d have to ask which one\u0027s better.)\\nThe most common"
      }, {
        "text": " correct answer is Premise or some other way of describing Supporting Idea.\\nEasier Role questions will stick to classic language"
      }, {
        "text": " like Premise and Conclusion, whereas harder Role questions will often switch to less familiar abstract language or synonyms.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Main Conclusion - The claim the author seeks to establish, a generalization supported by evidence, the only conclusion,\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " Premise - Support, partial support (just means there\u0027s more than one premise), evidence, illustration of, example"
      }, {
        "text": " of, unsupported premise\\n• Intermediate Conclusion - Subsidiary conclusion, supported premise, a conclusion but not the main conclusion,"
      }, {
        "text": " a statement for which some support is provided but is used to support the main conclusion\\n• Opposing Position - The"
      }, {
        "text": " view the argument is designed to discredit, the claim the author attempts to show is false\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n• Wrong Ingredient - These describe something in the paragraph, but not the claim we were asked about\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Too Strong - Some answers that about an idea being required or sufficient for the conclusion\\n• Didn\u0027t Happen"
      }, {
        "text": " - These reference something that never happened in the\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nparagraph\\n• Wrong"
      }, {
        "text": " Conclusion - Lots of wrong answers correctly call the claim a Premise but then say it\u0027s supporting some idea that doesn"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027t match the argument\u0027s Conclusion\\n\\nStrengthen\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMost"
      }, {
        "text": " strengthens, most supports, argument / reasoning / claim\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following,"
      }, {
        "text": " if true, most strengthens the argument?\\nThe reasoning above is most supported by which of the following?\\n\\nStim"
      }, {
        "text": "ulus Tendencies\\n\\n• Lots of causal arguments -- A Curious Fact (a correlation, an experiment, a"
      }, {
        "text": " survey, a statistic) followed by a Conclusion that makes a Causal Interpretation of that curious fact.\\n• Lots of"
      }, {
        "text": " comparative arguments -- The author will often assume \\\"Because A and B are similar in this way, they\u0027ll be similar"
      }, {
        "text": " in that way.\\\" Or \\\"because this was true before, it will be true again\\\".\\n• Many predictions /"
      }, {
        "text": " plans / recommendations -- These are also implicitly comparative, because we have to analyze all the potential differences between Present vs. Future"
      }, {
        "text": ", or Before-Plan vs. With-Plan.\\n• Not a lot of conditional logic.\\n\\nReading"
      }, {
        "text": " Goal\\n\\n1. Find the Conclusion.\\n2. Find the Support.\\n3. Ask yourself if"
      }, {
        "text": " you see any Logic Gaps or can think of anything the author is Assuming.\\n4. Consider how you would attack"
      }, {
        "text": " this argument if you wanted to argue the Anti Conclusion.\\n5. Think about what sort of evidence would make this argument"
      }, {
        "text": " more compelling.\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\nIf we hear something being assumed or sense a term"
      }, {
        "text": " shift or New Term in the conclusion, then we look for an answer connecting those ideas.\\n\\n• For C"
      }, {
        "text": "ausal arguments, primarily expect to add plausibility to the author\u0027s hypothesis (No Cause, No Effect data points,"
      }, {
        "text": " more Cause/Effect data points, or something else that explains how the causal connection would work or why it\u0027s a"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nlikely hypothesis), but we also might see correct answers ruling"
      }, {
        "text": " out an alternative explanation for the evidence.\\n• For Comparison arguments, we want to be more convinced that the two things"
      }, {
        "text": " being compared or similar (if the author is assuming they\u0027re comparable) or that the two things being compared are different"
      }, {
        "text": " (if the author is assuming they\u0027re different).\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n\\n• Do they We"
      }, {
        "text": "aken or feel like they\u0027re going Wrong Direction?\\n• Do they have No Impact (out of scope)?\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• Are they Too Weak? (some, sometimes, can, may, not all \u003d one data point)\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\n• Provides a Missing Link in the argument’s internal logic?\\n• Makes"
      }, {
        "text": " two things being compared seem more fair to compare? •\\nRules out an alternate explanation for a curious fact?\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Increases the plausibility of the author’s causal story? (#1 way - No Cause, No Effect way)\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• Makes a survey / sample / study / correlation seem more trustworthy or robust? (rare)\\n\\nWe"
      }, {
        "text": "aken\\n\\nKeywords\\n\\nMost weakens, most undermines, casts doubt on, fails to consider,"
      }, {
        "text": " ignores\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nWhich of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Which of the following, if true, casts the most doubt on Tom\u0027s reasoning?\\nThe argument is most vulnerable to"
      }, {
        "text": " criticism because it fails to consider\\n\\nStimulus Tendencies\\n\\n• Causal arguments -- A Curious Fact (a correlation"
      }, {
        "text": ", an experiment, a survey, a statistic) followed by a Conclusion that shows the Author\u0027s Causal Interpretation of"
      }, {
        "text": " that fact.\\n• Comparative arguments -- The author will often assume \\\"because A and B are similar in this way,"
      }, {
        "text": " they\u0027ll be similar in that way.\\\" Or \\\"because this was true before, it will be true again\\\".\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n• Predictions - What could be different in the future that would keep this prediction from coming true?\\n• Plan /"
      }, {
        "text": " Solution - How could this fail to achieve the goal?\\n\\nReading Goal\\n\\n1. Find the"
      }, {
        "text": " Conclusion.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n2. Find the Support.\\n3. Figure"
      }, {
        "text": " out how to argue the Anti-Conclusion, by framing the argument as\\n\\nGIVEN [this evidence], HOW"
      }, {
        "text": " CAN I ARGU E [the Anti-Conclusion]?\\n\\nPre-phrase Goal\\n\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " If we hear a Term Shift, we can look for an answer making those two things seem like they\u0027re not connected"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n• For Causal arguments, most correct answers suggest an alternate explanation for the Curious Fact in the evidence. Others"
      }, {
        "text": " will make the Author\u0027s Causal Story less plausible, often through a Cause / Effect mismatch (cause was present,"
      }, {
        "text": " but effect was absent / cause was absent, but effect was still present)\\n• For Comparison arguments, we will usually"
      }, {
        "text": " be asking ourselves, \\\"What could be a meaningful difference between these two things?\\\"\\n\\nIncorrect Answers\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n• Strengthen or drift in that direction\\n• No Impact - Out of Scope / Irrelevant Distinction / Ir"
      }, {
        "text": "relevant Comparison\\n• Too Weak - Words like some, sometimes, can, may, not all \u003d one data point"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nCorrect Answers\\n\\n• Attack a Missing Link in the argument’s internal logic?\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Make two things being compared seem meaningfully different? •\\nProvide an alternate explanation for a curious fact?\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " Undermine the plausibility of the author’s causal story?\\n• Cheapen the data from a survey /"
      }, {
        "text": " sample / study / correlation? (rare)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nReasoning Types"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nCausation\\n\\nBy Question Type\\n\\nSTRENGTHEN / WEAK"
      }, {
        "text": "EN / EVALUATE - Tons of arguments present a Curious Fact in the Evidence and then Conclude something Causal."
      }, {
        "text": " We end up needing to consider alternate storylines as well as the plausibility of the author\u0027s storyline.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "FLAW - When authors present a correlation or other Curious Fact and then overconfidently conclude that one thing causes another,"
      }, {
        "text": " we can criticize them for \\\"inferring a cause from a mere correlation\\\" or for \\\"failing to consider alternative explanations"
      }, {
        "text": "\\\". (There is a related trap answer that is never correct: \\\"Mistakes cause for effect\\\". It is okay to"
      }, {
        "text": " say the author has failed to consider that maybe she\u0027s got the causal picture backwards, but we aren\u0027t allowed"
      }, {
        "text": " to definitively say she got it backwards)\\n\\nNECESSARY ASSUMPTION - When the author concludes that \\\""
      }, {
        "text": "X caused Y\\\", she needs to assume \\\"there is not some other storyline for what caused Y\\\" as well as that"
      }, {
        "text": " \\\"X causing Y is a plausible storyline\\\".\\n\\nMUST BE TRUE / MOST SUPPORTED - Sometimes there are"
      }, {
        "text": " chains of causal connections (X causes Y. Y causes Z.), and so the correct answer will reward us for deriving the"
      }, {
        "text": " idea that \\\"X causes Z\\\" or that \\\"if we had less X, we\u0027d have less Z\\\". There"
      }, {
        "text": " are a lot of questions on Most Supported where the correct answer reinforces a Causal Difference-Maker. If we\u0027re"
      }, {
        "text": " told that X is true, and \\\"because of this / due to this\\\" Y is true, then the correct answer"
      }, {
        "text": " will synthesize/regurgitate that \\\"things like X can cause things like Y\\\". On some Most Supported questions, they"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027re playing a game of Solve the Causal Mystery, where they lay out a few factors and the correct answer offers"
      }, {
        "text": " the most conservative causal guess about how to explain this situation.\\n\\nPARALLEL FLAW - Not too many"
      }, {
        "text": " of these involve causality, but Parallel Flaw questions will occasionally test the Causal Flaw, where an author is too"
      }, {
        "text": " confident in her specific causal guess, even though other (potentially more likely) explanations are possible.\\n\\nPARADO"
      }, {
        "text": "X - Tons of correct answers will involve us needing some potential causal storyline for how two things could be different (or the"
      }, {
        "text": " same, depending). We\u0027re usually scanning the answers looking for some Causal Difference-Maker that would show why two"
      }, {
        "text": " different animals, or two different groups, or two different time periods, or two different situations end up looking differently.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nCausal Verbs\\nKeywords: causes, leads to, results in, has the effect of, promotes"
      }, {
        "text": ", inhibits, raises,\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nlowers, deters, encourages"
      }, {
        "text": ", influences, contributes to, is a factor in\\nCausal Difference-Maker Language\\nWhen these words start"
      }, {
        "text": " a sentence, it signifies that there\u0027s a causal connection between the previous sentence and this sentence.\\nKeywords: because"
      }, {
        "text": " of this, due to this, this allows, this leads to, this makes possible\\n\\nOn Most Supported"
      }, {
        "text": " tasks, it is legal to say, \\\"If the Causal Difference-Maker weren\u0027t present, the effect probably"
      }, {
        "text": " wouldn\u0027t have been present (or less of the effect would be present)\\\".\\n\\nCurious Facts"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nThese are almost all going to involve some sort of asymmetric comparison:\\n\\n• Ever since we raised the"
      }, {
        "text": " minimum sentence for burglary, there have been fewer burglaries.\\n• People who know how to play chess tend to be"
      }, {
        "text": " good at math.\\n• Subjects assigned to the group that heard Mozart were less likely than those who didn\u0027t to"
      }, {
        "text": " report being stressed by the newspaper headlines they read. • Knowing French is associated with having higher than average numbers of romantic partners"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n• Only 20% of the cars on the road are red, but 45% of speeding"
      }, {
        "text": " tickets are given to people who drive red cars.\\n\\nKeywords: correlated with, associated with, more likely to"
      }, {
        "text": ", less likely to, tend to\\n\\nCurious Fact → Author\u0027s Causal Story\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nThere\u0027s a huge swath of arguments in LR that fall into the same bucket:\\n\\n• The author"
      }, {
        "text": " presents some Curious Fact in the evidence. (a statistic, the results of a study, an asymmetric comparison)\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " The conclusion offers a Hypothesis / Explanation./ Interpretation\\n\\nThere are two different ways to affect a causal argument:\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n1. Propose or undermine Other Ways to explain the Curious Fact\\n2. Support or undermine the"
      }, {
        "text": " Plausibility of the Author\u0027s Story\\n\\nHere\u0027s how correct answers for causal arguments play out"
      }, {
        "text": " on various question types:\\n\\n• WEAKEN - They typically suggest some Other Way to explain the Curious Fact"
      }, {
        "text": " (an alternative explanation). Sometimes they undermine the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Story either by suggesting why it\u0027s"
      }, {
        "text": " an unlikely hypothesis or by providing data points that don\u0027t fit with the author\u0027s cause / effect relationship (Cause"
      }, {
        "text": " without Effect, or Effect without Cause)\\n• STRENGTHEN - They typically increase Plausibility of the Author\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s Story, particular by offering data points showing that where the Cause was absent the Effect was absent. Sometimes they rule out"
      }, {
        "text": " some Other Way to explain the\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nCurious Fact.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• FLAW - They say that it is a flaw to assume that X causes Y, just because they\u0027re correlated"
      }, {
        "text": " or just because that could be an explanation. Failure to consider alternative explanations for the evidence \u003d Causal Flaw.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• NECESSARY ASSUMPTION - They tend to rule out some Other Way, but occasionally they provide a missing link"
      }, {
        "text": " that allows the Author\u0027s Story to be plausible.\\n\\nConditional Logic\\n\\nImportance\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nAlthough this topic might affect less than 1/3 of the test, it\u0027s common for it to affect"
      }, {
        "text": " 1/2 or more of a student\u0027s incorrect answers. Conditional logic is found throughout LR and LG. In"
      }, {
        "text": " LR, there will often be conditional logic in the stimulus as well as the answer choices. In LG, around half of"
      }, {
        "text": " all games have at least one conditional logic rule.\\n\\nMost of us aren\u0027t very good at spotting or"
      }, {
        "text": " dealing with conditional logic. It\u0027s not something we\u0027ve had to use in our pre-LSAT life."
      }, {
        "text": " It often feels too hard, too mechanical, too mathematical. A lot of LSAT students are very good readers, very good at intuitive holistic thinking"
      }, {
        "text": ", and thus struggle with conditional logic unless they really learn some new skills and switch to those tools whenever conditional logic is on"
      }, {
        "text": " the menu.\\n\\nRelevant Question Types\\n\\nHere\u0027s where conditional logic will typically show up:\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n• Sufficient Assumption / Principle-Strengthen (stimulus and answers)\\n• Apply the Principle (stim"
      }, {
        "text": "ulus)\\n• Parallel / Parallel Flaw (stimulus and answers)\\n• Must Be True / Must Be False"
      }, {
        "text": " (stimulus and answers)\\n\\nHere\u0027s where conditional logic will often show up:\\n\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " Necessary Assumption (stimulus and answers)\\n• Most Strongly Supported (stimulus and answers)\\n• Flaw ("
      }, {
        "text": "if they\u0027re testing the Necessary vs. Sufficient flaw)\\n\\nThroughout these problems, look for keywords indicating conditional"
      }, {
        "text": " logic. Listen for claims with total certainty, or ones mentioning requirements. Jot down the \\\"X → Y\\\" version of these claims, and"
      }, {
        "text": " then also write the contrapositive.\\n\\n• (Put this on the LEFT): if, when, the only"
      }, {
        "text": ", all, each, any, every, no, whenever, whoever, wherever\\n• (Put this on the RIGHT): only,"
      }, {
        "text": " only if, requires, depends on, must, ensures,\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ng"
      }, {
        "text": "uarantees, implies, necessitates\\n• (Do an IF-NOT translation: put the attached idea on the"
      }, {
        "text": " left, but negated): unless, without, until, except perhaps\\n• No A\u0027s are B \u003d All"
      }, {
        "text": " A\u0027s are ~B\\n• Required things \u003d Right side of arrow\\n\\nIf you have more"
      }, {
        "text": " than one conditional, check to see if they chain together. You may have to write a contrapositive of one of them"
      }, {
        "text": " to see how they would connect. Conditionals chain together when you have the same idea on opposite sides, or opposite ideas"
      }, {
        "text": " on the same side.\\n\\nSame Idea / Opposite Side\\n\\n• A → B (B is"
      }, {
        "text": " on the right side)\\n• B → C (B is on the left side)\\n• Combined, it yields"
      }, {
        "text": " A → B → C\\n\\nOpposite Idea / Same Side\\n\\n• X → Y ("
      }, {
        "text": "Y is on the right side)\\n• Z → ~Y (~Y is on the right side)\\n• Combined"
      }, {
        "text": ", it yields X → Y → ~Z\\n\\nIf you have conditionals + facts, check to see whether"
      }, {
        "text": " any facts trigger the conditional or its contrapositive. Given a rule like, \\\"All unanimous votes occur under time constraints\\\","
      }, {
        "text": " it would look like this:\\n\\nUnanimous Vote → Time Constraint\\n~Time Constraint → ~Un"
      }, {
        "text": "animous Vote\\n\\nThe two triggers are Unanimous Vote and No Time Constraint, so we would examine any"
      }, {
        "text": " of the facts presented and see whether they mentioned there being either a Unanimous Vote or a lack of Time Constraint,"
      }, {
        "text": " which would trigger this rule and allow us to infer the right side.\\n\\nComparison\\n\\nAssumes"
      }, {
        "text": " a Similarity\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Linda loves 90\u0027s Hip-Hop, and she\u0027s really"
      }, {
        "text": " into Bubba\u0027s Nasty 90\u0027s Mix on Spotify. Thus, Jacob will probably be really into"
      }, {
        "text": " Bubba\u0027s Nasty 90\u0027s Mix too. Strengthen by making it seem like Jacob is also into"
      }, {
        "text": " 90\u0027s Hip-Hop. Weaken by making it seem like Jacob isn\u0027t into 90"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s Hip-Hop, or isn\u0027t into the stuff that Bubba plays on dat Nasty mix of his"
      }, {
        "text": ".\\n\\nEXAMPLE: LSAT students should never take full-length practice tests in the weeks leading up to taking"
      }, {
        "text": " an official LSAT. After all, taking an LSAT, like running a marathon, is a huge test of endurance"
      }, {
        "text": ", and marathon runners do not practice for their races by running full marathons in the weeks leading up to a race.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nStrengthen by making it seem like LSAT and marathons are fair to compare.\\n\\n------------------------------------------------"
      }, {
        "text": "--------------------------------\\n\\nWeaken by making it seem like there is something meaningfully different about LSAT taking and marathon"
      }, {
        "text": " running (maybe it\u0027s far easier for the brain to recover from the endurance test of an LSAT than it is"
      }, {
        "text": " for bones and muscles to recover from the endurance test of a marathon).\\n\\nAssumes a Difference\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nEXAMPLE: Paula and Jacoby have both never been able to complete a 720 on the half pipe before."
      }, {
        "text": " But Paula, unlike Jacoby, will probably be able to nail a 720 by the end of this vacation."
      }, {
        "text": " After all, she\u0027s currently working with a snowboarding instructor.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Jacoby is not"
      }, {
        "text": " also working with a snowboarding instructor.\\nWeaken by making it seem like Jacoby has done or is doing something that would"
      }, {
        "text": " match or exceed the benefit that Paula is getting from her snowboarding instructor.\\n\\nEXAMPLE: We just got back the"
      }, {
        "text": " results from the focus group on Presley\u0027s ad campaign. They found it, \\\"offensive to older people\\\". Thus,"
      }, {
        "text": " we should go with Marley\u0027s ad campaign instead.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Marley\u0027s ad campaign"
      }, {
        "text": " isn\u0027t offensive to older people.\\nWeaken by making it seem like Marley\u0027s campaign is just as offensive"
      }, {
        "text": " to the elderly, or point out some other detriment it has that would match or exceed the negative of Presley\u0027s ad"
      }, {
        "text": " campaign.\\n\\nPast vs. Present\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Paula and Jacoby have both never been able to"
      }, {
        "text": " complete a 720 on the half pipe before. Thus, by the time this vacation is over, they will"
      }, {
        "text": " have again failed to nail a 720 on the half-pipe.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like"
      }, {
        "text": " the past \u003d present in any other regard that might affect their performance. (they\u0027re in comparable shape, they\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "re comparably motivated, they\u0027re comparably uninjured, their understanding of snowboarding hasn\u0027t significantly improved).\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Weaken by making it seem like there\u0027s some important difference between before and now.\\n\\nPresent vs."
      }, {
        "text": " Future\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Over the past decade, the electorate has gotten increasingly polarized and radicalized by partisan media"
      }, {
        "text": " sources. Thus, ten years from now, we probably won\u0027t even be able to have conversations with people from another"
      }, {
        "text": " political party. Strengthen by making it seem like trends will continue or lean even more in the same direction. Weaken by"
      }, {
        "text": " making it seem like something important is going to change (Kanye wins the Presidency as a unity candidate), or something about"
      }, {
        "text": " the current trend is being overlooked (it\u0027s driven by ~80 year old billionaires who will soon be dead).\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nCurrent World vs. World of Plan / Recommendation\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Jasper has had a hard time connecting with his grandmother."
      }, {
        "text": " He should start watching more daytime soap operas.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nStrengthen by"
      }, {
        "text": " making it seem like this plan will work / it\u0027s feasible / it\u0027s targets a meaningful issue ... the grandma"
      }, {
        "text": " loves daytime soap operas and would be very enthused to talk about them with Jasper ... Jasper wouldn\u0027t have to sacrifice"
      }, {
        "text": " going to school in order to watch these daytime soaps.\\nWeaken by making it seem like this plan isn\u0027t"
      }, {
        "text": " feasible / people won\u0027t be willing to cooperate as the plan intends / the plan will backfire for some reason ..."
      }, {
        "text": " Jasper only has streaming TV and can\u0027t see satellite TV like daytime soaps .. Grandma only likes discussing soaps with her female"
      }, {
        "text": " friends ... Jasper and his grandma would have very conflicting opinions on what\u0027s going on in these shows, adding strain to"
      }, {
        "text": " their relationship.\\n\\nAnti-Causal\\n\\nEXAMPLE: Ivan and Crosby both updated their Tinder profile"
      }, {
        "text": ", but only Ivan updated his picture with a headshot done by a professional photographer. In the month following their profile updates"
      }, {
        "text": ", they each got around 100 matches. Therefore, Ivan\u0027s paying for that professional photographer was apparently a"
      }, {
        "text": " waste of money.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Ivan and Crosby were similar in any other meaningful regard (how"
      }, {
        "text": " attractive they are, how good/bad their personality description is, how many Matches they were previously getting).\\nWeaken"
      }, {
        "text": " by making it seem like Ivan and Crosby were not fair to compare from the start ... Ivan was starting from a lower reference"
      }, {
        "text": " point, and so achieving an equal number of matches to Crosby is actually showing that the pro photographer did make a difference ("
      }, {
        "text": "Ivan\u0027s less appealing / Crosby already had a pro photo for his pic / Ivan used to only get 40"
      }, {
        "text": " matches per month whereas Crosby had been getting 80 per month).\\n\\nPercentage vs. Amount\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nEXAMPLE: The average age of students at Hank\u0027s Ballroom Dancing School has decreased in recent years. Evidently, there"
      }, {
        "text": " are more and more young people rediscovering this timeless style of dancing.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like there are"
      }, {
        "text": " at least as many older people as there were before (rule out the possible objection that the average age went down not because"
      }, {
        "text": " younger people joined but because older people left)\\nWeaken by making it seem like the real reason that the average age"
      }, {
        "text": " has gone down is because there aren\u0027t as many older people or find a way to make it seem implausible that"
      }, {
        "text": " ballroom dancing is increasing in popularity among younger people.\\n\\nPros vs. Cons\\n\\nEXAMPLE: People"
      }, {
        "text": " who start smoking cigarettes regularly tend to have less appetite than they did before smoking. So, if you want to be healthier"
      }, {
        "text": ", try starting a cigarette habit so that you can lose some weight!\\nStrengthen by making it seem like the advantages"
      }, {
        "text": " to smoking outweigh the disadvantages.\\nWeaken by making it seem like even though smoking cigs might do one thing that"
      }, {
        "text": " could make you healthier (help you lose weight), it might have some negatives associated with it that would outweigh the advantages.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nEXAMPLE: The Baxter vacuum has more rug settings than The Suck"
      }, {
        "text": "ster. Thus, Reggie, who is in the market for a new vacuum, should buy the Baxter rather than the Suck"
      }, {
        "text": "ster.\\nStrengthen by making it seem like Reggie cares at least somewhat about having lots of rug settings, or by"
      }, {
        "text": " providing other ways in which the Baxter is equal to or better than the Suckster.\\nWeaken by making it seem"
      }, {
        "text": " like Reggie doesn\u0027t care about number of settings, or that most of the extra settings are dumb/useless,"
      }, {
        "text": " or by providing any sense in which the Baxter is worse than the Suckster (price, weight, convenience, etc.)"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nComplete the Analogy\\n\\nLots of examples of Logical Completion (i.e. fill"
      }, {
        "text": " in the blank) LR questions involve pulling together two strands of a conversation to complete a comparison. EXAMPLE: Singing an opera"
      }, {
        "text": " is just like running a marathon. Just as you wouldn\u0027t want to run a marathon without first stretching out your tendons"
      }, {
        "text": ", clearly ______ . Complete the analogy by making it seem like we should \\\"stretch out our tendons\\\" before we sing an"
      }, {
        "text": " opera (limber up our vocal cords?)\\n\\nExplain Curious Fact\\n\\nWhen Causation appears"
      }, {
        "text": " in the Assumption Family, it almost always involves an author speculating a possible Explanation to account for a given Curious Fact. We"
      }, {
        "text": " learn to consider alternate explanations and to evaluate the plausibility of the author\u0027s explanation.\\nThis type of argument is"
      }, {
        "text": " particularly common in Strengthen / Weaken / Evaluate, but it will also show up a fair amount on Flaw and Necessary"
      }, {
        "text": " Assumption.\\n\\nPrephrasing, by Question Type\\n\\nSTRENGTHEN\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "By ruling out an Alternate Explanation for curious fact\\nOR\\nBy increasing the plausibility of the Author\u0027s"
      }, {
        "text": " Explanation.\\nThe 2nd option is more common on Strengthen, and the most common type of plausibility strengthener is"
      }, {
        "text": " an answer that shows \\\"things not exposed to the cause do not exhibit the effect\\\".\\n\\nWEAKEN"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nBy suggesting an Alternate Explanation for curious fact\\nOR\\nBy lowering the plausibility of the"
      }, {
        "text": " Author\u0027s Explanation.\\nThe 1st option is more common on Weaken.\\n\\n----------------------------------------------------------------"
      }, {
        "text": "----------------\\n\\nEVALUATE\\n\\nThe correct answer choice asks a question whose answer would suggest an"
      }, {
        "text": " Alternate Explanation for the curious fact, or whose answer would establish a baseline of plausibility for the Author\u0027s Explanation."
      }, {
        "text": " The 1st option is more common.\\n\\nFLAW\\n\\nBy complaining that the author has"
      }, {
        "text": " over confidently assumed one specific causal relationship from evidence that could be interpreted different ways,\\nOR\\nBy complaining that the"
      }, {
        "text": " author has failed to consider an Alternate Explanation for the curious fact\\nOR\\nBy complaining that the author has failed"
      }, {
        "text": " to consider some fact that would hurt the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Explanation.\\nThe 1st option is more"
      }, {
        "text": " common on Flaw (just describing that the author\u0027s conclusion is too aggressively sure of itself, given the evidence).\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n\\nNECESSARY ASSUMPTION\\n\\nBy ruling out an Alternate Explanation for curious fact\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "OR\\nBy establishing some fact that\u0027s necessary for the Author\u0027s Explanation to be plausible.\\nThe 1"
      }, {
        "text": "st option is more common on Necessary Assumption.\\n\\nFind the Curious Fact\\nLook for an asymmetry between two"
      }, {
        "text": " groups or two situations or two timeframes. Look for a correlation or statistical change. Or just look for any funky fact"
      }, {
        "text": " that makes you scratch your head and think, \\\"why?\\\"\\nexamples\\n\\n• People who write poetry"
      }, {
        "text": " are more likely than those who don\u0027t to be in committed relationships\\n• Revenue at our company decreased by "
      }, {
        "text": "17% last week\\n• This year\u0027s craft fair had fewer macaroni sculptures than before • The purple catfish"
      }, {
        "text": " is known to circle around an area more than 100 times before deciding to lay its eggs there\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " In a study, a group of people who hate vegetables all had the same gene, XRV20, in common"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n\\nAsk Yourself the Curious Question\\nTurn these facts into questions.\\n\\n• Why is there"
      }, {
        "text": " a correlation between poetry and relationships?\\n• Why did revenue go down 17% last week?\\n• Why"
      }, {
        "text": " are there fewer macaroni sculptures this year? (or, why were there more macaroni sculptures last year?)\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n• Why does the purple catfish make 100+ circles before laying"
      }, {
        "text": " its eggs? •\\nHow come all the veggie haters have the same gene in common?\\n\\nSometimes the"
      }, {
        "text": " easiest way to find the Curious Question is to look at the Conclusion and picture the question that it\u0027s answering.\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "CONCLUSION: Apparently, Jim was drinking the night of his accident.\\nThe question must have been, \\\"Why did Jim get"
      }, {
        "text": " into an accident?\\\"\\nCONCLUSION: Scientists hypothesize that the crickets use this wax to fortify their nest.\\nThe"
      }, {
        "text": " question must have been, \\\"How is this wax helpful to crickets?\\\"\\n\\nArticulate the Author\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s Causal Storyline (Explanation)\\nMake sure to spell out every step in the causal sequence of the author\u0027"
      }, {
        "text": "s story. They frequently leave many parts of it implied.\\nMost people who scored an eventual 170+"
      }, {
        "text": " on their LSAT took a practice test on LawHub. Thus, if you want to get into a top law school"
      }, {
        "text": ", you should take a practice test on LawHub.\\nThe causal story here is something like, \\\"You don\u0027t"
      }, {
        "text": " have a 170 yet. A 170+ would be very helpful, if not necessary, to"
      }, {
        "text": " get into a top law school. If you take a practice test on LawHub, it will help you to raise your"
      }, {
        "text": " score to 170+.\\\"\\nPose the Two-Pronged Question\\n\\n1."
      }, {
        "text": " Is there some other way to explain the Curious Fact?\\n2. How plausible is the author\u0027s way?\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\nRemind yourself, based on the question type, whether you want the answer to suggest / rule out an Alternate"
      }, {
        "text": " Explanation, or whether you want the answer to increase / decrease the plausibility of the Author\u0027s Explanation.\\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "nCategorize the Correct Answers\\nWhen you\u0027re reviewing these questions, the skill you\u0027re trying to develop"
      }, {
        "text": " is the ability to speak this language:\\n\\\"Choice (B) is correct because it rules out an Alternate Explanation for"
      }, {
        "text": " why all the birds were lethargic.\\\"\\n\\nFamous Flaws\\n\\nWhere do we care"
      }, {
        "text": "?\\n\\nThese patterns are primarily of importance when it comes to Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions, although a"
      }, {
        "text": " couple of them (Causal and Nec vs. Suff) represent Reasoning Patterns (Causation and Conditional Logic) that"
      }, {
        "text": " permeate most of the LR section.\\n\\nWhy do we care?\\n\\nFlaw is the most prevalent"
      }, {
        "text": " question type in LR, and around 40% of the answer choices on Flaw refer to one of these ten"
      }, {
        "text": " famous flaws. If you wanted to memorize a handful more, then you\u0027d get around 13-15"
      }, {
        "text": " types of flaws worth memorizing. The primary value\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nof knowing them will be eliminating"
      }, {
        "text": " wrong answers. Most of the time we see these Famous Flaws in an answer choice, they\u0027ll be wrong."
      }, {
        "text": " But sometimes, maybe 35% of the time, the actual problem in the Flaw stimulus is one of these"
      }, {
        "text": " Famous Flaws.\\n\\nOn Parallel Flaw, we need to be able to either construct an abstract recipe of"
      }, {
        "text": " what the argument has done or to label it with some sort of Objection. For this latter category, the conversational objection we"
      }, {
        "text": " would think of is usually one of the top 6 Famous Flaws (Nec vs. Suff, Causal, Part"
      }, {
        "text": " vs. Whole, Sampling, Ad Hom, or Unproven vs. Proven False).\\n\\nTell me the Famous"
      }, {
        "text": " Flaws, already!\\n\\nHere is a description and an example of the top 10 Famous Flaws"
      }, {
        "text": " that show up in answer choices, followed by a few more that almost made the cut.\\n\\nNecessary vs."
      }, {
        "text": " Sufficient - Taking a conditional relationship and then illegally thinking through it in backwards or opposite fashion. This is frequently the actual flaw"
      }, {
        "text": " on Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions.\\n\\n• People who have seen \\\"The 13th\\\""
      }, {
        "text": " have a great understanding of the US prison system. Since Megan still hasn\u0027t seen that documentary, she must have a"
      }, {
        "text": " poor understanding of the US prison system.\\n• People who have seen \\\"The 13th\\\" have a great"
      }, {
        "text": " understanding of the US prison system. Jane understands the US prison system really well, so clearly she has seen \\\"The "
      }, {
        "text": "13th\\\".\\n\\nCausal - Overconfidently concluding one possible causal interpretation of a curious fact,"
      }, {
        "text": " usually a Correlation, when other possible interpretations exist.\\n\\n• People who have been enlisted in the military are more"
      }, {
        "text": " likely than those who haven\u0027t to have excellent physiques. Evidently, military recruitment officers select their future soldiers in large part based on the applicant"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027s fitness and strength.\\n\\nPart vs. Whole - Assuming that a trait that is true of each Part"
      }, {
        "text": " of a collective is therefore true of the Whole, or vice versa.\\n\\n• The military is part of the"
      }, {
        "text": " government, and the military is conducted with lots of honor and discipline. Thus, the government overall is conducted with lots of"
      }, {
        "text": " honor and discipline.\\n• Since rat poison is very harmful if ingested, each ingredient in rat poison would be very harmful"
      }, {
        "text": " if ingested.\\n\\nUnproven vs. Proven False - Pointing out that someone failed to prove X is true"
      }, {
        "text": ", and then concluding that this means that X is false.\\n\\n• Billy says that his uncle was related to"
      }, {
        "text": " Elvis, but he hasn\u0027t been able to connect them on Ancestry.com. Thus, Billy\u0027s uncle"
      }, {
        "text": " was not related to Elvis. •\\nSome claim that the Atlanta Braves should change their name because they are the only major sports team that"
      }, {
        "text": " still has a Native American mascot. However, the\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nChicago"
      }, {
        "text": " Blackhawks still have a Native American mascot. Thus, the Braves should not change their name.\\n\\nAd Homin"
      }, {
        "text": "em - Dismissing a point of view because the source has a biased interest or has conflicting past behavior.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• My friend Sameer says that social workers would be better at responding to domestic abuse calls than would armed police officers."
      }, {
        "text": " But we can ignore this notion, since Sameer is a social worker and so his industry would benefit were cities to make"
      }, {
        "text": " this switch.\\n• My governor is telling me that we should all wear masks now, but we should not heed her"
      }, {
        "text": " words. After all, she was vocally against wearing masks a month ago.\\n\\nSampling - Extrapol"
      }, {
        "text": "ating/generalizing too readily from a small sample, while failing to consider that the sample might be biased, too small, or un"
      }, {
        "text": "representative.\\n\\n• Charlie, a staunch Republican, is from San Francisco and works for a theater company. Since"
      }, {
        "text": " Sheila is also from San Francisco and also works for a theater company, she is probably a staunch Republican too.\\n•"
      }, {
        "text": " Today\u0027s youth prefer comic books to literature. After all, in a survey conducted at last year\u0027s Comic-"
      }, {
        "text": "Con, over 85% of the young people surveyed reported liking comic books much more than novels.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "There are several things you can do, in complementary fashion, to increase your facility with Famous Flaws:\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Flip through a bunch of Flaw and Parallel Flaw questions, mainly scanning the answers on Flaw to see if"
      }, {
        "text": " you recognize any of these famous flaws, or by reading through the arguments to see if any of them seem like they are"
      }, {
        "text": " committing one of these flaws.\\n• Create your own flashcards (potentially reversible ones), where you either see the name"
      }, {
        "text": " of a famous flaw and quiz yourself on what an answer choice describing that flaw would sound like, or vice versa (you"
      }, {
        "text": " read the answer choice and quiz yourself on whether you can name the flaw)\\n• Write your own example set for these"
      }, {
        "text": " flaws. For the flaws that have more than one style, try writing an example for each style. Send them to another"
      }, {
        "text": " LSAT student, or post on Reddit, or email Laura / Matt / Patrick if you want some feedback on them.\\"
      }, {
        "text": "n----------------------------------\\nIrrelevant Rebuttal - Rebutting someone\u0027s claim or argument by mischaracter"
      }, {
        "text": "izing it in some Straw Man way or by bringing up something wholly irrelevant to the original claim.\\n\\n• My"
      }, {
        "text": " neighbor complains about how loud my music is, saying that the bass frequencies rattle all her furniture. But she must be lying"
      }, {
        "text": ", because I only play country music, not rock.\\n\\nFalse Choice - Acting like you have to choose between"
      }, {
        "text": " two options even though you never established those are the only two options.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\n• We could use Grandma\u0027s birthday money to buy the new Mario Kart. But we could also use it"
      }, {
        "text": " to buy a rope swing for the yard. Since we can\u0027t find Mario Kart available anywhere online, we\u0027ll"
      }, {
        "text": " have to buy the rope swing.\\n\\nRelative vs. Absolute - Getting mixed up with Percents vs. Amounts, or confusing more/"
      }, {
        "text": "less language with yes/no language.\\n\\n• Some say that older people are never great at video games,"
      }, {
        "text": " but they\u0027re wrong. After all, Grandma is much better than Uncle Toby at Mario Kart\\n\\nIn"
      }, {
        "text": "appropriate Appeal - Your evidence is a plea for sympathy, you cite an \\\"authority\\\" without establishing credibility, or you favor"
      }, {
        "text": " one source\u0027s opinion over another source\u0027s, without justifying your pick. Almost always wrong answers:\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• Elon Musk tweeted that concerns about the impact of 5g radiation are conspiracy theories, so we needn’t worry"
      }, {
        "text": " about renting this house next to a 5g cell tower. (Appeal to Authority)\\n• I know I was"
      }, {
        "text": " speeding, Officer, but you shouldn\u0027t give me a ticket because it would ruin my Anniversary. (Appeal to Emotion"
      }, {
        "text": ")\\n• The majority of voters in Country A don’t believe rising sea levels pose a problem for their country,"
      }, {
        "text": " so we should abandon legislation that would protect the coast against what local climatologists say is a looming threat. (Appeal to"
      }, {
        "text": " Popular Opinion)\\n\\nCircular Reasoning - The premise of an argument restates or assumes the conclusion.\\n\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "• The LSAT is clearly the hardest grad school test, because all the other tests people take to go to grad school"
      }, {
        "text": " pale in comparison to the difficulty of the LSAT.\\n\\nInternal Contradiction - Something the author says undermines"
      }, {
        "text": " or contradicts something else they say\\n\\n• LSAT is an incredibly challenging test for anyone who takes it."
      }, {
        "text": " However, if you commit to Eddie Spaghetti\u0027s Steady LSAT Readiness protocol, you will soon be feeling like LSAT"
      }, {
        "text": " is no harder than a kindergarten spelling quiz.\\n\\nEquivocation - Using the same \\\"term\\\" or concept"
      }, {
        "text": " to mean two very different things.\\n\\n• Some question whether Egypt condones Romania\u0027s recent military buildup on"
      }, {
        "text": " their eastern border. But clearly Egypt sanctions this activity, since in the weeks following the Romanian military\u0027s actions, Egypt"
      }, {
        "text": " issued sanctions against Romania.\"}"
      }, {
        "text": "",
        "thoughtSignature": ""
      }],
